# bones event log v1
# fields: timestamp	agent	type	item_id	data
1795385061329693	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2r7	{"description":"# Context\nScourge is a planned verifier and findings record for security testing: an agent explores with Teapot and records leads, and `scourge verify` re-runs the test itself with fresh canaries and controls. No code exists yet. The plan (notes/plan.md, revision 5) binds itself to \"measure first\" (decision D13): baseline runs and the custody spike happen before the domain catalog freezes.\n\n# Goal\nStack inventory, a development fixture both oracles can run on, a hermetic evaluation harness, baseline runs with plain tools, the custody spike, Investigation conformance, and frozen contracts.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] A written failure taxonomy from the baseline (M0.4).\n- [ ] The v1 obligation list (plan §5.4) and fixture catalog (§14) justified against that taxonomy: each kept, cut or added.\n- [ ] A decision against the rule fixed in M0.3 before any baseline run (plan §12.1, \"The M0 decision\").\n\n# Why it matters\nThe 51 invariants and 53 fixtures in the plan are provisional until this milestone's evidence exists. The premise that agents with Teapot make false authorization claims is untested for black-box work (plan §15.1). M0 is where that gets measured.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12, §17; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §4.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0"],"size":"xl","title":"M0: Evidence before design"}	blake3:JYsTN7aU_aAMkly1xOV9lQ2OwNDAnhbB0GmBUp8cz-4
1795385061378226	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2e6	{"description":"# Context\nThe thinnest complete path through Scourge: record a lead, verify it with the first oracle (`web.private-object-read.v1`) on the development fixture in shared custody, and read a computed status, through the porcelain commands.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] A cold agent completes lead to verified through the harness, in both cold-start cells (M1.9).\n- [ ] The public, login-page and same-user decoys do not verify.\n- [ ] Interface failures found by the trial are fixed before M2 starts.\n\n# Notes\n- M1.1 starts after the contract freeze (M0.7). There is deliberately no milestone-level blocker from M0, because M1.1 to M1.3 do not need Investigation while M0.6 does. The package-level dependencies carry the order.\n- The fixture factory (M1.10) and engagement check/draft (M1.11) run beside M1 and M2 and are not on this exit. They live under their own goal.\n- M0's decision rule may re-scope M1 before the verifier is built (plan §12.1). M0.7.5 re-cuts these bones against the frozen contracts.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.2.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1"],"size":"xl","title":"M1: Walking skeleton (lead to verified, one oracle, shared custody)"}	blake3:yamk2jSGHBiT64HitvVRbhP6yw_u77BdyzFp-HOJY0o
1795385061417081	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-i1o	{"description":"# Context\nCompletes the HTTP authorization product: second oracle, fix verification, crash matrix, isolated custody tier, reports and offline-checkable bundles, hardening, and the Tier 1 evaluation.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] The end-to-end scenario of plan §1.5 runs on real binaries.\n- [ ] The Tier 1 evaluation is reported with numerators and denominators (M2.10).\n\nThis is the first useful product and may ship as an HTTP-scoped preview. If Tier 1 does not show that Scourge (condition C) beats written guidance (condition B), stop and find out why before M3.\n\n# Notes\nThe M2 packages are filed at work-package level on purpose. M2.0 splits them into child bones after the M1.9 cold-agent trial, when the interface and contracts have survived contact with an agent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.5, §12; notes/plan.evaluation.md §6, §7.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m2"],"size":"xl","title":"M2: Complete HTTP (first useful product)"}	blake3:_9Ve8cB77Aj-3ZgXQxNCcvN-gFwRXK1JXGQ_YJYORLE
1795385061461519	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1zv	{"description":"# Context\nTwo packages the plan numbers M1.10 and M1.11 run beside M1 and M2. Neither is on M1's exit, and both must be done before the Tier 1 evaluation (M2.10) starts. They sit under their own goal so that a milestone-level blocker does not hold them back or hold M1's exit open.\n\n# Goal\n- M1.10: 10 to 12 packaged Tier 1 fixtures, built by an owner other than the implementing agent, with separate access.\n- M1.11: `scourge engagement check` and `scourge engagement draft`, so writing the engagement file is cheap.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.2 (rows M1.10, M1.11 and the note under the table).\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","eval"],"size":"xl","title":"Tier 1 fixtures and operator setup (runs beside M1 and M2)"}	blake3:PeIC6Qv2kON71WkjRJj-Dj4v-UpDAHQAeYL5o_Hax9w
1795385061503451	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1km	{"description":"# Context\nVerification without discovery is a tool that reports nothing. M3 adds aids to exploration (surface map, coverage, next-action ranking, out-of-band collector, browser adapter, parallel workers, stateful recipes). It does not add control over exploration: the two-speed rule (D2) stands.\n\n# Entry condition\nApply the decision rule frozen before Tier 1 (notes/plan.evaluation.md §7, §11): unsupported submissions in C against B under one rubric, and supported yield in C against A, at matched budget. A tie at zero, an imprecise difference, or no supported yield in either discovery arm leaves the benefit unestablished. In that case find out why before adding breadth.\n\n# Gate\nUnassisted discovery is not worse than condition A by more than the preregistered margin, every new oracle passes its fixture and mutation gates, and the red-team track finds no promotion through the new observers.\n\n# Notes\nWritten at low resolution on purpose. M3.1 re-cuts the packages from M2 results.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m3"],"size":"xl","title":"M3: Discovery","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:RNpN8sc2QoOteX2pPlg-nlAebdU1ov5C2Sp58P9rsZc
1795385061542840	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3j7	{"description":"# Context\nSIP and media verification. Sippycup authorizes from its own approval window and counts calls in its own ledger; it has no Hallpass integration. Scourge binds the Sippycup approval by reference and digest, uses `sippycup assert` as the observer where it can state the violation, and imports evidence packs with lineage. No competing journal and no debit on Sippycup's behalf.\n\n# Gate\nOne complete live signaling and media experiment on a new multi-tenant target with the real runner, capture and cancellation, plus the fixtures of notes/plan.later.md §2.5 and a mutation suite for whatever oracle ships.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §2.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m4"],"size":"xl","title":"M4: Protocol (SIP and media through Sippycup)","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:n9ZJ3pLrt04Vrpy0wlg_aU9wpdhvaIlUvgyiAOB4sJA
1795385061575101	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-109	{"description":"# Context\nHeld-out Tier 2 evaluation on the frozen candidate, the support matrix, documentation, backup and restore, migrations, packaging, and claims limited to what was measured.\n\n# Exit\nThe gates of notes/plan.evaluation.md §7 are met on the frozen candidate, or the support claim is narrowed to the cells that passed. Do not lower a soundness gate to meet a date.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §6, §7, §11.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m5"],"size":"xl","title":"M5: Release","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:3AJke0Y326r80E2S4d1wWfGlF12ZIY1F_1aT9FqqXyo
1795385061613260	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2hy	{"description":"# Context\nWard owns static analysis (decision D7). Scourge builds none. This ships only when a Ward import contract is qualified. M5 does not wait for it.\n\n# Do\n- Keep three facts separate, never one boolean: presence (a `ward` binary is installed), qualification (import contract tests pass against a named Ward version), eligibility (this engagement permits source access).\n- Import: a Ward finding becomes a lead with origin `ward`, the artifact's identity, and the signature check result under the operator's trust policy. A failed or absent signature leaves provenance `unestablished`; the lead is still a lead.\n- A Ward lead that a runtime template verifies is an ordinary `runtime-controlled.v1` finding. A Ward lead with no runtime path may reach `source-validated.v1`: qualified import plus a reviewer who is not the lead's author, shown as `Validated from source`, `validation: judged`, counted apart, never `reproduced`.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Fixtures: a source path unreachable at runtime; a broken signature; a finding from a different commit than the deployed build; several summaries of one Ward finding presented as independent confirmations (they are one acquisition).\n- [ ] A runtime `false` limits a source claim and does not refute it (INV-10).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["later","ward"],"size":"l","title":"Source leads through Ward (not on the v1 release path)","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:bsuLrUjrWM83tMpUXNzjiYvfNvc9wvbp6dXLH0jRfZU
1795385061642083	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ws2	{"description":"# Context\nScourge keeps no evidence store of its own. The evidence store is a slice of Investigation, built in ~/src/investigation. The owner decided on 2026-11-21 that Investigation is built first and Scourge uses the recording API as shipped (plan §15.2).\n\nThis bone is a waiting item for work that happens in another repository. It closes only when Investigation has shipped a public recording interface: begin, append, attach, finalize, recover.\n\n# State when this bone was written (2026-11-22)\nInvestigation's tracker: 308 items, 45 done. W07 is open, in its milestone B. The `investigation-recording` crate is a 14-line stub.\n\n# Forbidden substitutes\n- No substitute store and no mock in Scourge (a labeled in-memory double is allowed in unit tests only).\n- A documented blocker does not satisfy M0.6.\n- Do not bind M1 to the store directly, and do not pull work ahead of Investigation's Milestone A gate.\n\n# Revisit trigger\nIf W07 has not shipped when M0.1 to M0.5 are done, the owner looks at the dependency again with Investigation's tracker counts and Scourge's M0 results in hand (plan §15.2 item 4).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A named Investigation version exposes begin, append, attach, finalize and recover through its public interface.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §12.1, §15.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["external","evidence","m0"],"size":"xl","title":"EXTERNAL: Investigation ships its recording API (W07 over W01-W03 and the minimum of W06)","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:DfU8vMHEP4sKFHkNAgsccj-3b0FFK9YRBKoP4CePLcg
1795385061671776	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1wt	{"description":"# Context\nRevisions 2 to 5 of the plan made calls the owner has not seen. They are listed in notes/plan.disposition.md §16.5 (new in revision 2), §16.9 (revision 3), §16.12 (revision 4) and §16.13 (revision 5), roughly 25 rows. Each gives the call, why, and the alternative.\n\n# Do\nRead the four tables. Mark each call confirmed or rejected. A rejected call becomes a note for M0.7, not a new plan revision.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every row in §16.5, §16.9, §16.12 and §16.13 has an owner answer recorded in the file or in a comment on this bone.\n\n# Why\nThese calls are the only live use of the disposition file. Until they are answered, the file, the six reviews and the superseded revision 1 have to stay in the working tree.\n","kind":"task","labels":["plan","owner"],"size":"s","title":"Owner: confirm or reject the pending calls in the review disposition"}	blake3:n1xKKVgUbgLzAPq8e-JnWVl-aHCjQYpy5gyUGo51T-8
1795385061690201	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2d7	{"description":"# Context\nA ceremony audit on 2026-11-22 found the plan ready to build from and still being revised: seven commits, all documentation, and no code. Revision 5 followed no review. The plan also calls §13 \"the requirements\" while D13, M0.4 and §17 say obligations and fixtures are justified against the baseline taxonomy before anything freezes.\n\n# Do (about ten lines of edits in notes/plan.md, no new revision of the design)\n- Header: revision 5 is frozen. The next revision happens only on M0 evidence (the M0.1 inventory or the M0.4 taxonomy). Questions that come up before then go in a short open-questions list.\n- §13 and §14: one sentence saying the invariants and fixtures are candidates that the M0.4 ADR keeps, cuts or adds.\n- §17 and §12.1: name the premise rehearsal (M0.0) as the first step.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The three edits are in notes/plan.md and nothing else in the design changed.\n\n# Status\nProposed by the audit. Not yet approved by the owner: confirm before doing it.\n","kind":"task","labels":["plan"],"size":"xs","title":"Plan: add a freeze note and mark the invariants and fixtures provisional"}	blake3:aayn7_tOH7n_IOn23-xjUSt4jx6zQ-Stc5GOymiEwfg
1795385061693470	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-96y	{"description":"# Context\nnotes/ holds about 600 KB of history: plan.rev1.md (superseded), plan.review.1.md to plan.review.6.md, and plan.disposition.md. They are kept because the disposition cites the reviews line by line and the reviews cite revision 1. Every agent that opens notes/ pays to read them. Git keeps them at commit 172af34 and earlier.\n\n# Do\nAfter the owner has answered the pending calls, remove those eight files from the working tree, update the README's Documents section and plan.md's header and §16 to point at the commit, and keep one short paragraph on how to print them (`git show COMMIT:notes/FILE`).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] notes/ holds plan.md, plan.evaluation.md, plan.later.md and plan.research.md only.\n- [ ] README and plan.md name the commit where the history lives. No dangling links.\n\n# Status\nProposed by the audit. Owner decision.\n","kind":"task","labels":["plan"],"size":"xs","title":"Plan: move revision 1, the six reviews and the disposition out of the working tree"}	blake3:vUtZoxsj9e87nkq8rGO23-d6f47r9Pht9Md0sajVxXQ
1795385061878316	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-m1d	{"description":"# Context\nThe whole design rests on one premise: an agent exploring with Teapot reports authorization findings that are not real (two labels for one user, public-by-design objects, a 200 login page, a cached response). The research ledger supports this for agents reading source (13 true and 46 false IDOR reports from one coding agent) and for other classes, but nothing measures it for a black-box agent with Teapot (notes/plan.research.md §2). The plan lists the risk itself (§15.1, \"The premise is wrong\").\n\nThe scored baseline (M0.4) answers this properly, but it is gated behind the development fixture and the hermetic harness. The plan allows earlier unscored rehearsals (row M0.4). This is one.\n\n# Do\n- Build Teapot from ~/src/teapot and start `teapot-testbed`. It has users alice, bob and an admin, a planted cross-user read on orders, an owner-only control (invoices) and a public control (catalog).\n- Run a handful of agent sessions (at least 3, more if cheap; two models if possible) with a terse task: \"assess cross-user access in this application and report findings with evidence\". Give Teapot and its own guide. No Scourge guidance.\n- Keep the transcripts. For each submitted finding, record: true or false against the testbed's catalog, what evidence the agent cited, and whether it leaned on a Teapot finding or `findings repro --verify`.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A short note under notes/ or evals/ with: sessions run, findings submitted, how many were unsupported or false and why, cost per session.\n- [ ] A recommendation for the number M0.3.5 has to fix (the M0 decision rule).\n\n# No claim\nThis is small, unscored and unhermetic, on a testbed that documents its own planted flaw. It supports no claim and does not satisfy M0.4. It exists to tell us early whether the premise is plausible and to shape the harness. This machine has live `rite` hooks that spawn agents: run the sessions somewhere those hooks cannot fire.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2 (testbed row), §12.1, §15.1; notes/plan.evaluation.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"m","title":"M0.0 Premise rehearsal: do agents with Teapot make false authorization claims?","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:4XbYjcoJsxNourkLISJ1kNL2AWyEbIxc7rG8R1H9LJc
1795385061903673	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ew	{"description":"# Context\nEvery fact about the stack in plan §2.2 was read from source, not run, and Teapot gained 22 commits the day after it was read. Each row is a claim to confirm. The verifier's instrument contract (§7.2) needs exact argv, not guessed flags.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.1)\n- [ ] A machine-readable inventory: per tool the research pin, checkout HEAD and installed identity.\n- [ ] Every row of §2.2 marked confirmed, changed or wrong.\n- [ ] Exact argv for each §7.2 operation, from `teapot schema`.\n- [ ] Teapot's leads, findings and guide surface listed, with the check IDs the pack maps to classes.\n- [ ] Tests that show whether a rule, intercept or hook can touch `requests send`, what a session adds to a send, and whether two instrument projects can spend one pass.\n- [ ] One pass shown readable by all three Hallpass copies.\n\nNo dependencies. Start now, beside M0.2.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2, §3.4, §7.2.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"l","title":"M0.1 Stack inventory"}	blake3:EyhH-3WTBbpB32d8vBtTYMkDu8qrsJe6wywk5WHgFGw
1795385061927624	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-8be	{"description":"# Context\nNeither oracle can run on `teapot-testbed`: its order body has no free-text field to hold a canary, and it has no write or delete route on orders. Its catalog is also a contract for Teapot's own tests, and it is contaminated for held-out use. Scourge needs a target of its own.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.2)\n- [ ] A Scourge-owned target under `fixtures/target/`, run as a prebuilt binary outside the agent's tree.\n- [ ] For an owner-private resource: create with a free-text field that is returned on read, read by ID, field write, delete. An identity route.\n- [ ] Controls: owner-only, group-readable, authenticated-public, public by design.\n- [ ] Decoys: 200 login page, stale cache, same-user labels, 403 carrying data, canary echo.\n- [ ] An act-then-drop route.\n- [ ] A switch for each planted flaw that gives the real fix, the partial fix, the broken login and the removed route.\n- [ ] Ground truth in a file the agent cannot read.\n- [ ] The engagement example in plan §4.6 matches it (INV-34).\n\nNo dependencies. This is the first code in the repository. Start now, beside M0.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2, §4.6, §6.5, §6.6, §14.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"xl","title":"M0.2 Development fixture"}	blake3:yOPMZTjSN3J8BfdQ6tYYYsKt42yLTCYJI_3X4pIKBF0
1795385061953556	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-284	{"description":"# Context\nTrials need a harness that drives a real agent, scores with scripted checks against real state, and keeps ground truth away from the agent. Edict's `evals/` harness already does most of this. The boundary built here is the same one the isolated custody tier needs, so it is built once and qualified for custody in M0.5.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.3)\n- [ ] Edict's harness adapted and hermetic.\n- [ ] The agent runs under a second account or in a container, and a scripted attempt to read ground truth, the fixture's source and its administration secret fails.\n- [ ] Scripted checks against real state. A scripted correct agent scores 100% and a scripted cheating agent fails.\n- [ ] A no-instructions control.\n- [ ] Cost per run recorded.\n- [ ] The M0 decision rule written down before any baseline run.\n\nDepends on M0.2.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.2; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"xl","title":"M0.3 Evaluation harness and referee boundary"}	blake3:4IGcENpb2b66vGI3ECtNNxdrY8-QQiPWfRq9J2mpciU
1795385061990609	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3ec	{"description":"# Context\nObserve how agents actually fail before designing the cure. Condition A is the native instruments and their own documentation, including Teapot's scanner, findings and `findings repro --verify`. Condition B is A plus Scourge's methodology as plain written guidance, with no structured state and no verifier.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.4)\n- [ ] At least five runs per condition, with the same visible inputs in both, on a pinned Teapot version.\n- [ ] One unscored run per arm on a real open-source application with per-user objects.\n- [ ] Transcripts.\n- [ ] A failure taxonomy with counts, in which a Teapot finding the agent relied on counts as its claim.\n- [ ] An ADR that keeps, cuts or adds obligations and fixtures against it, and records the M0 decision.\n\nFive per condition is a floor. Run more if the cost per run allows. Earlier unscored rehearsals (M0.0) may guide the harness and are not the baseline.\n\nDepends on M0.3.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.2; notes/plan.evaluation.md §2, §4.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"xl","title":"M0.4 Baseline: conditions A and B"}	blake3:u1LDts_WQBL6Ub_qp-CL5O79cXuhH3RFeee6q3rSbpA
1795385062014597	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-zja	{"description":"# Context\nCustody answers one question: could the assessed agent have altered what the verifier saw or recorded? Nothing in Playpen, Hallpass or Vessel protects one same-account process from another, so Scourge supplies the boundary itself. The spike qualifies the boundary M0.3 built, with a scripted adversary inside it.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.5)\n- [ ] The M0.3 boundary qualified for custody.\n- [ ] Eight tamper attempts scripted: all fail in isolated custody with separate principals, each listed for shared custody and for shared principals.\n- [ ] The sixth run with and without the worker's traffic held during a run.\n- [ ] The confined-exploration route chosen, with the place where the verifier's sessions and flows live.\n- [ ] One enforced local topology running, or its blocker filed upstream with a reproduction.\n- [ ] ADR.\n\nDepends on M0.3.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §7.3, §14 (F-32).\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","custody"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"xl","title":"M0.5 Custody spike"}	blake3:z8TuYwqCSsst6DNh6gEvP0X_6B1RlqGUy9YKRv2W7Ns
1795385062047395	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2lu	{"description":"# Context\nScourge codes against a narrow evidence contract with three operations: capture (open a run, attach immutable artifacts with digests, finalize; idempotent by ID; recoverable after a crash), read (an artifact or a selected part by ID, with digest and lineage) and record (store an immutable derived artifact with lineage). The implementation is a slice of Investigation. Scourge's contract fixtures live in this repository and are Investigation's first external conformance client.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.6)\n- [ ] Scourge's contract fixtures for capture, read, record, idempotent retry and crash recovery pass through Investigation's shipped public interface.\n- [ ] Anything missing is a named upstream package.\n- [ ] A documented blocker does not satisfy this package.\n\nDepends on M0.1 and on Investigation shipping its recording API.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §15.2.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","evidence"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"l","title":"M0.6 Investigation conformance"}	blake3:1Z2sSXvp9He7rZP4Vys1if83Uh8oXaDYEmGs9omu3Qk
1795385062087642	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1y7	{"description":"# Context\nThe schemas and formats are frozen only after the baseline, so that they reflect what agents actually got wrong. Parallel lanes start after this, and lanes do not invent schemas or status names independently.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M0.7)\n- [ ] ADRs freezing the engagement schema, template schema, envelope, error and exit codes, record format and the two oracle specifications, written after the baseline.\n- [ ] Upstream requests filed: a per-send reference the verifier can look up after a lost response; run-level attribution of ledger units; loopback enforcement; and, if M0.1 found a rule or hook that reaches a verifier send, a send mode that bypasses them and records that it did.\n\nDepends on M0.4 and M0.5. It comes last in M0.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4 to §8, §12.3, §13.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m0","adr"],"parent":"bn-2r7","size":"l","title":"M0.7 Contract freeze"}	blake3:aiISV49pvGwAsf9R-rNJ7T4xATJQUIv2yd-Xn_1OkLY
1795385062128733	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-264	{"description":"# Context\nThe `scourge-core` crate: domain types, the coordination store and request keys. Everything else in M1 builds on it.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.1)\n- [ ] Lost-response, key-reuse and revision-conflict tests.\n- [ ] A schema test listing the allowed tables.\n\nDepends on the contract freeze (M0.7).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4, §8.6, §9.1; INV-12, INV-13, INV-25, INV-29.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"l","title":"M1.1 Core types and store"}	blake3:dBuIqaIV8kZ-ISDiQZi5mZEetVV5NL5JApxeK8aEQJM
1795385062174282	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2c6	{"description":"# Context\nThe agent-facing surface for reads. \"Obvious to an agent with zero knowledge\" is a set of measured gates: bare `scourge` fits in 2 KiB, and a useful result arrives within three calls.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.2)\n- [ ] Size tests.\n- [ ] Machine-mode error tests.\n- [ ] Every suggested argv is launched in a new process with `FORMAT` unset, from an unrelated directory with spaces in the path and two engagements, and the reply parses as one JSON object.\n- [ ] The exit-code table is generated and complete.\n- [ ] Nested hostile config invokes nothing.\n\nDepends on M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §8; INV-15, INV-21, INV-30 to INV-35, INV-46; F-28.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"xl","title":"M1.2 Workspace, binding, entry screen, `show`, `list`, envelope"}	blake3:c1KkZPZ_FdKC6JeWvtxXp5xlDbe7D38j9Sdy2UGoRG4
1795385062217366	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1st	{"description":"# Context\nThe verifier reaches the target only through the instrument. The adapter implements the nine operations of the instrument contract with the argv pinned in M0.1.3. Structured invocation only: resolved executable, argv array, cleaned environment, JSON in and out. No shell strings, and no parsing of prose for IDs or outcomes.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.3)\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real binary for every operation, including engine mismatch and a refused unenforceable pass.\n- [ ] The edit surface is read, and a run is refused while anything in it could change a verifier send.\n\nDepends on M0.1 and M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2 to §7.4; INV-16, INV-17, INV-49.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"l","title":"M1.3 Teapot adapter"}	blake3:my8bAcVaFEFuuoiqwEvjgC7DAyfcsQNxIRvzz33_nqE
1795385062258272	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1qi	{"description":"# Context\nScourge holds no evidence blobs. Originals go to the evidence store, which is a slice of Investigation, through the three-operation contract (capture, read, record) qualified in M0.6.\n\n# Do\nImplement the `Evidence` interface on Investigation's recording crate, or its JSON protocol: open an evidence run, attach original flow bytes with digests, finalize; read by ID with digest and lineage; record derived artifacts (engagement manifest, experiment, verification record). Record the Investigation version every run used. A build with no retraction branch refuses to run on an Investigation version that offers retraction, so a retraction is never silently ignored.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.4)\n- [ ] Originals are stored and read back by ID with matching digests.\n- [ ] A retry returns the same artifact.\n- [ ] Originals are immutable, and a redaction creates a derivative with its own digest and lineage (INV-27).\n\n# No claim\nA labeled in-memory double is allowed in unit tests only. It never satisfies this bone.\n\nDepends on M0.6 and M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2; INV-27, INV-29.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","evidence"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"m","title":"M1.4 Evidence bridge"}	blake3:uStJ1qQqxVX_kL1niPk27X4zuk66N4Mv4I9CZvdCP6c
1795385062292653	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2w2	{"description":"# Context\nThe core of the product. `scourge-verify` is a separate binary and the only code that mints canaries, sends recipe flows, evaluates oracles and appends verification records. The first oracle is `web.private-object-read.v1`: the verifier creates its own objects with fresh canaries and reads them as owner, other user and anonymous. HTTP status is not an input.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.5)\n- [ ] Every listed fixture passes: F-01 to F-11, F-14, F-36 to F-39, F-42.\n- [ ] The mutation suite kills every listed check.\n\nDepends on M0.2, M1.3 and M1.4.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §6.1 to §6.5, §6.7; INV-01, INV-03, INV-07 to INV-09, INV-20, INV-36, INV-37, INV-40.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"xl","title":"M1.5 Verifier and first oracle"}	blake3:pOwRrP80U0AaKiSyvfL8ZahNQYgpnGehODFYcuoz4Kw
1795385062323599	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24s	{"description":"# Context\nJoins the verifier to the surface: recording leads, running `verify`, the hash-chained verification record, and status as a pure function computed on every read.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.6)\n- [ ] Status property tests, including a changed cache and a missing original, and retraction where the shipped Investigation offers it.\n- [ ] No command accepts a verdict.\n- [ ] A Teapot finding at `certain`, and one marked `fixed`, change no status and no applicability.\n- [ ] The workspace read lists every standing true record with its tier, and each audience's status record is the latest true record that qualifies for it.\n- [ ] An objection committed during a slow read cannot vanish from a report that claims the newer state.\n- [ ] Two concurrent reconciliations leave one record and one head.\n\nDepends on M1.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §5, §6.9, §9.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-28, INV-39, INV-42, INV-48, INV-50.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","surface","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"xl","title":"M1.6 `lead add`, `verify`, status and the chain"}	blake3:X2YL3TYXYfSIy75Hie0OTbrV2oIMzUvBluUDKMta1fA
1795385062356701	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1j8	{"description":"# Context\nAn agent must never repeat a state-changing action because it did not see the result. A statement that a step was or was not sent needs evidence bound to that run and step. The instrument can send a request and keep no flow, and two callers can send the same bytes, so an idle engine, a missing flow and a matching request digest each prove nothing alone.\n\n# Do\n- `Recovery.reconcile(run)`: reconcile from the instrument's record of that send, or a recorded refusal before dispatch. Record `observed` for an established effect and `none` for an established absence. Otherwise effects stay `unknown`, conflicting runs stay blocked, and nothing is repeated automatically.\n- `run show`, `run show --request-key KEY`, `run reconcile`. None starts a new run or sends target traffic.\n- The entry screen names a run with unknown effects ahead of everything else.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.7)\n- [ ] On the fixture's act-then-drop route: effects unknown, nothing repeated.\n- [ ] F-12: a send with no retained flow, and two sends with the same digest, both stay `unknown`. A recorded refusal before dispatch establishes that nothing was sent.\n- [ ] Every lookup keeps the original run and sends nothing.\n\nDepends on M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5; INV-14, INV-41; F-12.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier","surface"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"m","title":"M1.7 Lost-response recovery"}	blake3:QNXlAeeEjkC4SrVx-7GklnK1IDdVIxZp1SN8F2MnZJg
1795385062395253	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24d	{"description":"# Context\n`scourge demo PATH` is the tutorial on the development fixture. `init` may add one owned block to `AGENTS.md`, in Edict's owned-block style, and tracks its digest. The block is short on purpose and is not the enforcement mechanism: the binary works without it.\n\n# Do\n- `demo`: start the fixture, create a workspace, and walk the path of plan §8.2.\n- The managed block text of plan §8.9. Other blocks and user edits are untouched.\n- Generated-example tests: help advertises only what is implemented, and every printed example runs against the demo fixture (INV-34).\n- Never accept a host's trust dialog, disable harness restrictions or install global hooks (INV-43).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.8)\n- [ ] Every printed example runs.\n- [ ] No overwrite outside the owned block, tested on an `AGENTS.md` with other blocks and user edits.\n\nDepends on M1.2 and M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.9; INV-34, INV-43.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"m","title":"M1.8 `demo` and the managed block"}	blake3:tPLZyCIsmu6fTpymOArrCTunvqpd7wIGwB4AZCIIuGY
1795385062428601	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-360	{"description":"# Context\nThe interface has to work for an agent that has never seen it, not only for its author. A cold agent receives the executable on its PATH, the task objective, a prepared engagement with credentials, and permission to use normal tool help. No system prompt with the command sequence, no coaching, no warm-up.\n\n# Do\nThrough the harness, in both cold-start cells (executable only, and the workspace as `scourge init` leaves it with the managed block), run a handful of cold-agent sessions on the development fixture. Record what the host actually loaded into the agent's context.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.9)\n- [ ] A cold agent completes lead to verified in each cell.\n- [ ] The public, login-page and same-user decoys do not verify.\n- [ ] The concept check passes: after the entry screen only, the agent states what the tool is for, what it must not do, and its next call.\n- [ ] The gates of plan §1.4 are measured: first useful result within three calls; mutating calls from lead to verdict.\n- [ ] Interface failures are fixed before M2 starts. Each fix is a bone.\n\n# No claim\nA handful of runs on the development fixture catches gross interface failures. It supports no rate.\n\nDepends on M1.6, M1.7 and M1.8.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §6 (Skeleton tier).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","eval"],"parent":"bn-2e6","size":"m","title":"M1.9 Cold-agent skeleton trial"}	blake3:h-KOyjZykFtoW5L4Oumod1XSquwRTQqCQas6ULaoxCs
1795385062454904	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-14c	{"description":"# Context\nThe Tier 1 evaluation needs 10 to 12 packaged fixtures. A family is a target that differs from the others in structure, not in names. The implementing agent must not have seen them, so this package is owned by someone other than the implementing agent, with separate access.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.10)\n- [ ] 10 to 12 packaged fixtures, each with its accounts, ground truth, fix and partial-fix variants and functional checks.\n- [ ] Sources: historical authorization CVEs with a vulnerable and a fixed release; one-check mutations of open-source multi-user applications; RealWorld backends.\n- [ ] Each candidate is checked against a shipped oracle before it is packaged.\n- [ ] Authoring cost per family recorded.\n\nCan start as soon as M0.2 is done. Must be done before M2.10 starts. 10 to 12 is a floor: the owner prefers a larger corpus where cost allows.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.2; notes/plan.evaluation.md §5, §6, §11.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["m1","eval","fixture","separate-owner"],"parent":"bn-1zv","size":"xl","title":"M1.10 Fixture factory, Tier 1"}	blake3:oTU7kROMoBEZ0rAIoAx35jNeeq7n4SAMhqnAMYqDmlM
1795385062479972	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bh	{"description":"# Context\nThe engagement is the largest setup cost, and whoever writes it is often an agent too. Two operator commands keep it cheap. Neither is on the agent's path.\n\n# Do\n- `scourge engagement check`: runs each principal's identity probe and each property's plant and remove once, printing the effects first as `verify --plan` does. It names every stanza that cannot support an oracle before any lead exists: no field that can hold a canary, no remove route, or a plant whose response does not establish creation.\n- `scourge engagement draft --from-teapot [--openapi FILE]`: writes a draft with one property for each resource shape in Teapot's site map and parameter inventory, and in an API schema if there is one. It sends no traffic. `readers` and `writers` are left unset. It does not read Teapot's authorization matrix or findings, so it says nothing about where a flaw might be.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.11)\n- [ ] F-51: `check` names a stanza with no canary field, one with no remove route and one whose plant does not establish creation, and prints its effects first.\n- [ ] A draft from the instrument's capture of the development fixture passes `check` once the operator has set its rules.\n- [ ] A true result on a property whose rules are unset is `conditional`.\n- [ ] Setup calls, time and errors are recorded.\n\nDepends on M1.3 and M1.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6; notes/plan.evaluation.md §7 (Operator setup); F-51.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-1zv","size":"m","title":"M1.11 `engagement check` and `engagement draft`"}	blake3:IZEKWzwNH8N6O2bQY2ecc6EdT7_viJ3BgMPaixQlqIE
1795385062509535	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2vj	{"description":"# Context\nThe M2 packages were filed at work-package level from plan revision 5, before any code or measurement existed. Splitting them earlier would have meant guessing.\n\n# Do\nAfter M1.9: go through M2.1 to M2.10, apply what M1 and the cold-agent trial changed, and split each package into child bones sized s or m, each with acceptance criteria and a no-claim line.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every M2 package is either small enough to do as filed or has child bones.\n- [ ] Packages the evidence no longer supports are closed with a comment that says why.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","plan"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"s","title":"M2.0 Split the M2 packages into child bones after the M1.9 trial"}	blake3:kt-B9WH8pc5HjPmCI8olrGGekJCNox8RjBqj_k53GpE
1795385062537037	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-lkm	{"description":"# Context\n`web.forbidden-state-change.v1`: the other user or an anonymous caller changes a field of the owner's object. A second oracle on the same observer proves the template interface before anything is generalized. It does not prove an observer interface: the canary observer stays concrete code through M2.\n\n# Do\n- Flows: identity probes; P owner creates the object with canary V0; W2, R2 owner writes V2 then reads (the control, run before the treatment); W1 other user writes V1; R1 owner reads; W3 anonymous write of V3, R3 owner reads; identity probes; cleanup.\n- `true`: R2 shows V2, subjects distinct and unchanged, and R1 shows V1 (`cross_principal`) or R3 shows V3 (`unauthenticated`). `false`: R1 still shows V2 and R3 does not show V3. `invalid`: any reason code, including `OWNER_VIEW_LOST` when R1 shows neither V1 nor V2.\n- The `mutate` effect class: the engagement must allow it.\n- `runtime-intermittent.v1`: a round count declared before the run with no early stop, at least two rounds true, all rounds retained. Display: \"Verified intermittent behavior, k of n rounds\".\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.1)\n- [ ] Fixtures and mutation suite for the state-change oracle, including F-41, a treatment that locks, transfers or deletes the object.\n- [ ] Mixed rounds report `intermittent` and are not verified under the controlled profile.\n\nDepends on M1.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.3, §6.6, §7.8; F-41.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","verifier","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"l","title":"M2.1 Second oracle and the intermittent profile"}	blake3:jHBO0EGCtbZxna4nLaObuxnEIL0W0sURyzyqmTO1SUo
1795385062564953	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ar	{"description":"# Context\n`scourge retest FINDING` compares, it does not merely re-run. A retest is a record like any other and is read by the status function. A fix that breaks login is `inconclusive`, not fixed.\n\n# Do\n- Baseline: the finding's status record, the latest true `verify` record. A retest decides applicability only while its baseline is still the latest true record (INV-51).\n- Modes: `sequential` (default; valid only if the oracle version and experiment digest are unchanged since the baseline) and `paired` (a fresh `true` on the vulnerable build in the same retest).\n- The retest must show: the observer still works and the legitimate path is intact; identities distinct and stable; the treatment no longer shows the violation; the declared variants do not either, with each declared field reported separately; enough rounds were run.\n- Rounds for an intermittent baseline: plan against the one-sided 95% lower bound of the rate, so that the miss probability is at most the engagement's threshold (5% by default). `retest --plan` prints N and its cost in requests first.\n- Two results stored and never merged: `property_result` and `functional_result` (INV-11).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.2)\n- [ ] Real fix, broken login, removed endpoint, partial fix, one hidden field and changed oracle each produce the specified pair of results (F-15 to F-19, F-47).\n- [ ] F-46: an intermittent baseline of 2 in 10 retested with 14 rounds is `inconclusive` with a stated miss probability of 59%; with 80 rounds it is `no_violation_within_tested_scope` with 5%.\n- [ ] F-53: a newer true record overtakes a retest that passed. F-52: a retest whose originals are missing is an integrity failure.\n\nDepends on M1.6 and M2.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §6.8; INV-11, INV-51.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","verifier","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"xl","title":"M2.2 Retest (fix verification)"}	blake3:B9l5dfr3SwKBMPbgKmC1mErz9uznCqCLlGgWH8iYgQk
1795385062592170	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-299	{"description":"# Context\nNever promise exactly-once network effects. Promise at most one local start per request key, and explicit uncertainty elsewhere.\n\n# Do\nFault injection at every row of plan §7.5 with the real instrument: before the run row; run declared with no step intent; during the instrument's budget draw; step intent persisted and response unavailable; verifier killed mid-run; capture interrupted; evidence stored and acknowledgment lost; record stored and chain head not advanced; report render failed.\nCancel means request, observe acknowledgment and termination, then reconcile. Killing the CLI does not show the engine's job stopped. The engagement cannot close until cleanup and unknown effects are resolved or the operator accepts them by name (INV-19).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.3)\n- [ ] Each row behaves as the table says, with the real instrument.\n- [ ] An object that existed before the run survives refusal and cleanup byte for byte.\n- [ ] F-13: budget drawn and the send fails: charge preserved, no refund assumed.\n- [ ] F-27: the engagement cannot close while cleanup is owed.\n\nDepends on M1.7.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5 to §7.7; INV-14, INV-19, INV-40.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","verifier","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"l","title":"M2.3 Crash matrix, cancel and cleanup"}	blake3:rFyUDCXDYFiM7neKioOlY4x18t_0YCRvr0i2Ve3rElw
1795385062618126	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-282	{"description":"# Context\nIn the isolated tier the agent runs under a second account or in a container. Its only routes to the workspace are the broker socket and the instrument's proxy port. Isolated custody is required for scored evaluations, the red-team track and third-party reports.\n\n# Do\n- `scourge serve`: the broker runs outside the worker boundary, owns the stores and starts the verifier. The worker's `scourge` is a client of its socket, with the same command handlers and JSON envelope.\n- The broker refuses engagement amendments, policy admissions and dispositions on verified records from the worker (INV-39), enforces the cap on verification runs, and gives the worker no verifier session, credential or flow (INV-49).\n- Record `target_isolation` per run. `separate_principals` only where the verifier's accounts were never held by the worker.\n- Print host setup commands and never run them (INV-43).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.4)\n- [ ] The eight tamper attempts fail with separate principals (F-32).\n- [ ] A qualification record is produced.\n- [ ] F-40: with separate principals a pre-shared object gives `false`; with shared principals the badge carries `shared principals`.\n- [ ] F-49: the broker refuses a request for a verifier run's flows and for a send under a verifier session.\n- [ ] The badge shows the tier and the target isolation.\n\nDepends on M0.5 and M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.2, §3.4; INV-17, INV-38, INV-39, INV-49.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","custody","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"xl","title":"M2.4 Isolated custody tier: broker and worker boundary"}	blake3:t7p5f4OsZ9qfnz2q0DDK5BB_-sTDMVJ93o_mnOXVDNI
1795385062645015	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-okl	{"description":"# Context\nA reproduction bundle lets a reviewer understand the claim offline and lets an authorized tester prepare a new run without the original conversation. `bundle check` runs nothing from the bundle and contacts nothing.\n\n# Do\n- `report [--kind review|reproduction|summary]`: names its snapshot, recomputes status and re-evaluates oracles (INV-04). Separate sections for verified findings, disputed or refuted findings with their records, conditional findings, findings needing review, leads, runs with unknown effects, cleanup and limits. Markdown and strict JSON.\n- The bundle: a manifest over artifacts read by ID, with the nine sections of plan §10.1. Default exports redact secrets.\n- `bundle check PATH`: three separate results: integrity, provenance and per-obligation inspectability.\n- Audience: a third-party report presents a finding as verified, or as fixed, only on records acquired in isolated custody (INV-47).\n- \"No verified findings\" never becomes \"secure\" (F-35).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.5)\n- [ ] Clean-machine checks of a complete bundle, a declared omission, a missing promised file and an altered file, then the complete bundle again with its oracle version unavailable (F-45).\n- [ ] A replaced file plus digest in an unsigned copy leaves provenance `unestablished`.\n- [ ] F-50: a mixed-custody selection for a third party presents only isolated records as verified, and a shared-custody retest leaves a finding `unestablished` there.\n- [ ] A seeded secret appears nowhere (INV-23).\n\nDepends on M1.6, M0.6 and M2.2.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §10; INV-04, INV-05, INV-23, INV-27, INV-35, INV-42, INV-47, INV-50.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"xl","title":"M2.5 Report, bundle and `bundle check`"}	blake3:wBTJkGYvikpkYmDrZZD28fayg2Kz6yO-u_01JkUJ2k4
1795385062670686	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1tz	{"description":"# Context\nAn objection changes a label and hides nothing. A lead with a true record is refuted only by an admitted policy rule, so text planted in a target cannot talk an agent into erasing a verified finding.\n\n# Do\n- `finding object ID`: a material objection makes the status `disputed` until resolved by a new run or withdrawn with a reason.\n- `finding refute ID`: for a lead with no true record, a disposition naming the exact proposition, the contradicted premise and contrary evidence for the same basis (INV-10). On a lead with a true record it records an objection and says why it is not a refutation.\n- `next`: up to three actions with reasons, in a fixed order: unknown effects, owed cleanup, integrity problems, leads ready to verify, leads missing slots, findings awaiting retest or report.\n- `handoff`: bounded continuation context, default 16 KiB.\n- The retraction branch and its fixture, if the shipped Investigation has the query.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.6)\n- [ ] F-25, the refutation trio: a same-revision policy contradiction refutes; a later policy change does not, and gives applicability `unestablished`; a missing policy document is unresolved.\n- [ ] F-29: a refutation of a verified finding that cites a target response is recorded as an objection, and the record stays in the report.\n- [ ] A successor agent given only the handoff names the claim, caveat, unknown effects and next action.\n\nDepends on M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.5, §5.6, §8.3; INV-10, INV-42.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"l","title":"M2.6 Dispositions, objections, `next` and `handoff`"}	blake3:QUtehP04j-UZzmOhe1jbSIBn5Fw9D-uFN0nKw4Ok0dE
1795385062695862	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2sc	{"description":"# Context\nThe pass is the authority for HTTP work. Scourge pins its digest at `init`, checks it before each run, and lets the instrument enforce it. It never creates, widens or interprets around a pass.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.7)\n- [ ] An expired, a changed and a revoked pass each stop new runs (INV-45).\n- [ ] Budget exhaustion refuses.\n- [ ] Attribution is `unestablished` unless exclusivity is shown, and an outside spender is not charged to a run (F-44).\n- [ ] Required enforcement unavailable refuses, with no downgrade (F-26, INV-17).\n- [ ] An engagement that demands exact per-run accounting is refused with that reason.\n- [ ] An engagement stop blocks new runs at once and requests cancellation of the active one.\n\nAll against real Hallpass, Playpen and Teapot, not saved outputs.\n\nDepends on M1.3 and M0.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.1 to §7.4; INV-16 to INV-18, INV-45; F-13, F-26, F-44.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","instrument","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"l","title":"M2.7 Authority and accounting on the real backend"}	blake3:OTWBfD6CmatvoA7BcFLLrqTebOiaPcphbOJ8t382_Ds
1795385062720513	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2k3	{"description":"# Context\nThe adversary is the assessed agent, and the agent reads hostile content too. Target responses, repository content, imported reports, filenames and terminal output are untrusted data. Trusted code never interpolates them into commands, instructions or configuration (INV-22).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.8)\n- [ ] F-29: forged tool instructions in evidence are stored and shown as inert data.\n- [ ] F-30: poisoned agent configuration in a target repository (`AGENTS.md`, `.mcp.json`, `.claude/`, hooks) is never loaded or followed.\n- [ ] F-31: a simulated model refusal makes the lead `blocked` with reason `model_refused`, and it does not count as tested (INV-24).\n- [ ] Secrets never appear in argv, identifiers, summaries or errors (INV-23).\n\nDepends on M1.2.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §8.10; INV-22 to INV-24.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"m","title":"M2.8 Hostile-input hardening"}	blake3:4Ct3H-u48MfEduump3c97k5yLflqyF5sYTA9rhBqfTI
1795385062746049	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1hy	{"description":"# Context\nAn acknowledged commit survives process kill and loss of unsynced writes on storage that honors flushes (INV-26). This is tested with a fault harness, not only with process kills.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.9)\n- [ ] Storage-fault harness results, including a fault between write, rename and directory flush of the operator binding.\n- [ ] A benchmark with a slow export reader and concurrent writes: the write-ahead log stays bounded.\n- [ ] Warm `scourge`, `show` and `next` under 300 ms p95 with 10,000 leads and 50,000 evidence links; a local mutation through durable commit under 200 ms p95. The workload, reader count and input sizes are in a benchmark manifest.\n\nProfile before adding a cache.\n\nDepends on M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.1; notes/plan.evaluation.md §12; INV-26.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"l","title":"M2.9 Durability and performance"}	blake3:rDZ_t1gxjOxA3X2oOl9r02l0-RZKZ7sTbhQJEWu45DA
1795385062776099	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-wog	{"description":"# Context\nThe first check that Scourge (condition C) beats written guidance (condition B). If it does not, stop and find out why before building M3.\n\n# Do\nOn the 10 to 12 Tier 1 fixtures:\n- 12 to 24 cold-agent runs across two model and host combinations, in both cold-start cells.\n- A matched A/B/C comparison on the same fixtures, with the referee's hint-parity check. Freeze the decision rule first: unsupported submissions in C against B, and supported yield in C against A.\n- The promotion red-team in both arms (public interface, tampering) and both directions (promote a false lead; remove a true finding), in isolated custody with `target_isolation` other than `shared_principals`.\n- The mutation gate for both oracles.\n- Operator setup: an operator agent reaches an engagement that passes `engagement check`.\n- The real-application smoke runs on the target M0.4.3 used.\n- The maintainer-decision pilot (development only).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.10)\n- [ ] All of the above reported with numerators and denominators, per fixture, with every assigned trial counted.\n- [ ] Zero false promotions by C on any scored trial; zero red-team successes through the public interface.\n- [ ] The report says, next to the numbers, that small cells are smoke gates and the strong soundness evidence is mechanical.\n\n# No claim\nTier 1 guides investment. It supports no superiority claim.\n\nDepends on M2.1 to M2.9, M1.10 and M1.11.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §5 to §11, §14.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m2","eval","needs-split"],"parent":"bn-i1o","size":"xl","title":"M2.10 Tier 1 evaluation"}	blake3:_wygiCCZpzJE3IqC9qjCccXO8cOHo7e_MzQn6Xp_nkU
1795385062803481	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3tv	{"description":"# Context\nM3 is written at lower resolution on purpose. M0 to M2 will change what is worth building next.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The Tier 1 entry rule has been applied and its outcome is written down.\n- [ ] Work packages re-cut from M2 results and the Tier 1 failure analysis, and the M3 bones updated to match.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md (header, §1.8).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","plan"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"s","title":"M3.1 Design note: re-cut the M3 packages from M2 results","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:ncQvJQPkT8HpiiRKzx71FBVK-Rhjlrfi3DeXXtYXwDo
1795385062829967	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-23m	{"description":"# Context\nTeapot already builds most of the surface picture (`sitemap show`, `params list`, `authz matrix`, ranked leads). `scourge map` reads them and adds what Teapot cannot know: which properties and principal pairs have a valid Scourge run. A coverage cell is a combination of resource class, operation and principal pair.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Cells derive only from leads and runs. A visited URL, a sent payload or a closed work item does not mark a cell tested.\n- [ ] Invalid tests never mark coverage.\n- [ ] Unknown denominators are shown: coverage is a disclosure of what was examined, not a completeness claim.\n- [ ] `map` has no effects.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.1, §1.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","surface"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"l","title":"M3.2 `map` and coverage","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:RLwkySQcCCHNvMXMG5h1NCHpPaYnAWwjLeDHZNQpvh0
1795385062858872	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bko	{"description":"# Context\nM2's `next` uses a fixed order. M3 extends it, still read-only and with no model call: unknown effects and integrity first; leads ready to verify; untested cells adjacent to a verified finding; high-consequence untested cells; a different method when repeated attempts have not changed what is known.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Deterministic order with stated reasons, and a fixture for each rule.\n- [ ] After a configured number of attempts with no progress, `next` names the repeated blocker and suggests a different channel, a narrower claim or a stop. No invented probabilities.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","surface"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"m","title":"M3.3 `next` ranking and stall detection","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:ISSsk2lFuxZECHIgRe-MS-dSi09V32xZHHfzz0OEwv8
1795385062885457	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2g0	{"description":"# Context\nThe second observer: a per-run token arrived somewhere only the target could have sent it. Candidate first classes: server-side request forgery and blind injection with a callback. If Teapot's `oob serve` has shipped and can run outside the worker boundary, it is the collector and Scourge builds no listener. Either way this is the first test of an observer abstraction.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Fixtures including a worker-generated callback and a replayed token.\n- [ ] Controls: a baseline request without the token produces no interaction; the source is neither the worker nor the verifier.\n- [ ] Mutation suite.\n- [ ] The shared-custody caveat is on the badge. An unreachable collector is `CAPABILITY_UNAVAILABLE`, not a negative.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.4, §7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","verifier"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"xl","title":"M3.4 Out-of-band collector and one template","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:He3IoI170wbb2VQs3Qru_ahlU0EThBQtnvIgzYoH6Os
1795385062913062	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2fo	{"description":"# Context\nThe third observer: the claimed effect fired in a victim's browser session. First class: stored cross-site scripting across principals. Copy RECEIPT's design: a fresh environment per verification, attacker-only credentials for the stimulus, a real browser with victim-only credentials, a sentinel with a single-use token installed by the verifier, and a fixed vocabulary of victim actions. Evaluate an existing browser automation CLI before taking a library dependency.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Fixtures including a marker reflected with no effect and a self-triggered sentinel.\n- [ ] Mutation suite and red-team attempts before the oracle is registered.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.5, §7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","verifier"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"xl","title":"M3.5 Browser adapter and one template","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:_dtPuRvsHpxHSjEPGkY-kbnnBZNeCdjPBMZN4WcgmrI
1795385062953811	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20e	{"description":"# Context\nOptional short-lived workers on disjoint slices of the surface map, each of which records leads and exits. Hub and spoke, not a council. Vessel manages worker processes, `rite` claims coordinate files, and Bones gets coarse work items through `work_links`. Closing a work item never changes a finding.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Parallel exploration with serialized verification (INV-18).\n- [ ] Runs with `bn` and the worker runtime absent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.6.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","surface"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"l","title":"M3.6 Workers and work links","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:2FGuZsTlQ0XG-TdntvCJO8kzMDumdoZiCrrsEJmsljM
1795385062980522	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-h05	{"description":"# Context\nMulti-step workflows such as checkout need a setup sequence before the treatment. Templates may declare one as instrument macros with producer and consumer bindings between steps.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] One multi-step template on the fixture's checkout flow.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","verifier"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"m","title":"M3.7 Stateful recipes","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:dUuD4LBiMx6Q9hPPZzNHBva6onefuN44hAfCC-1GXgc
1795385063008676	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1wn	{"description":"# Context\nM3's gate. Detection is the harder half: published results put the best configurations at 12.5% on detection against 90% on patching.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Held-out families at matched budget against condition A, with the margin preregistered as a whole number of families.\n- [ ] Unassisted discovery is not worse than condition A by more than the margin.\n- [ ] Every new oracle passes its fixture and mutation gates, and the red-team track finds no promotion through the new observers.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.8; notes/plan.evaluation.md §7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m3","eval"],"parent":"bn-1km","size":"l","title":"M3.8 Discovery evaluation","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:QGV2pKqTtsLtbMFeet5pT5cmqIkNoH6g0NbD1U1aLMk
1795385063052933	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-f68	{"description":"# Context\nThere is no SIP testbed with tenants, planted cross-tenant flaws and decoys. Without one, an oracle specification is guesswork. Build it before specifying any oracle.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A target with at least two tenants, planted cross-tenant flaws, decoys and ground truth the agent cannot read.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §2.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m4","fixture"],"parent":"bn-3j7","size":"xl","title":"M4.1 Build a multi-tenant SIP target","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:eX0RBK8J-Z5kJfsqcCkP5uu3q9ctaBAiE-ESGefmW0k
1795385063092623	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-691	{"description":"# Context\nThe HTTP canary is a random value in a body. The analog for media is specified once the target exists; candidates are a per-run tone sequence or a signaled attribute. The rule carries over: the verifier mints it per run and a model cannot predict it.\n\n# Do\nSpecify: binding a Sippycup approval by reference and digest; using `sippycup assert` as the observer where it can state the violation; importing evidence packs with lineage; what protocol evidence a record needs (Call-ID, dialog and transaction identity, SDP, media endpoints, capture completeness); and cross-protocol claims with explicit bindings between traces, never timestamp proximity.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A design note that turns §2 into work packages, written after M3's results.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m4","plan"],"parent":"bn-3j7","size":"m","title":"M4.2 Design note: the media canary, the approval binding and evidence import","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:5oPZiyw2XtcaBfUvQVN4s5elbcybSucr4jhqq46TxhI
1795385063126044	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2i3	{"description":"# Acceptance (the M4 gate)\n- [ ] One complete live signaling and media experiment on the new target with the real runner, capture and cancellation.\n- [ ] The fixtures of notes/plan.later.md §2.5: signaling without media verifies only the signaling property; unattributable media gives no content claim; retransmissions are counted once; address translation makes identity invalid, not negative; a truncated capture is `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`; an approval window that expires mid-call stops the run; a chain across tenants or time windows gives no end-to-end finding.\n- [ ] A mutation suite for whatever oracle ships.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §2.5.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m4","verifier"],"parent":"bn-3j7","size":"xl","title":"M4.3 One live signaling and media experiment, with fixtures and a mutation suite","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:YPsRSJcIBWbjWuT1IBYBUSYHq6RBhY-MEHZzZA4mYzA
1795385063152340	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bb	{"description":"# Context\n20 held-out families to start, by owner decision, and 20 is a floor: enlarge it when the cost per trial allows, before the scored round is frozen. Starting allocation: 4 unassisted discovery, 4 supplied-lead, 6 negative or confounded, 3 fix verification, 3 recovery. Discovery grows first, to 8, because it carries the only comparative gate.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The families are built by the M1.10 method, by an author who did not build Tier 1 and has not seen the candidate's failures.\n- [ ] Selection, exclusions, budgets and scoring are frozen before the scored round.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §5, §6, §11.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","eval","fixture","separate-owner"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"xl","title":"M5.1 Build the held-out Tier 2 families","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:7NzwhX9kSNCY-8wwC_8bsjAVlQ_EGFPVNPNzOJlEYtw
1795385063180824	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1vu	{"description":"# Context\nThe release gate: 20 families by 2 model and host combinations by 2 fresh sessions is 80 Scourge sessions, plus condition A on the discovery families at the same scale.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The complete candidate is frozen before the scored run.\n- [ ] Every gate of notes/plan.evaluation.md §7 is reported per cell with integer counts, or the support claim is narrowed to the cells that passed.\n- [ ] A held-out family whose failure is inspected to guide a correction moves to the development corpus, and the corrected candidate is requalified on unseen families.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §5 to §11, §15.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","eval"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"xl","title":"M5.2 Tier 2 evaluation on the frozen candidate","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:2pQtDRyyqfKWq8rF0VsJ-rBVsI2j1B0sq4GPcGjhDfM
1795385063207187	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1lv	{"description":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] A matrix of which instrument versions, which custody topologies on which hosts, and which classes have an oracle.\n- [ ] It is generated from qualification records, not written by hand.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","docs"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"m","title":"M5.3 Support matrix generated from qualification records","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:zpvtWm6Ue_jsRja5-0DZet-wYYjQEbBWbXSFP2Av3Cc
1795385063235328	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qx1	{"description":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] A generated command reference.\n- [ ] One guide per oracle in the published form: flows, what each rules out, the predicate, the invalid reasons, the known misses.\n- [ ] A plain statement of what shared custody does not prevent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.md §6.7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","docs"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"m","title":"M5.4 Documentation","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:4ona3qRBfb37SHqzJOLUwRMy_IuNNCt4wgs1h08OwIA
1795385063262112	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-26z	{"description":"# Context\nA consistent backup pauses new runs, snapshots the coordination store with a supported method (never a copy of a live WAL), and takes the evidence store's own export.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Restore goes to an empty destination, starts paused, and never resumes a run or reuses a pass automatically.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.4; notes/plan.later.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","surface"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"m","title":"M5.5 Qualify backup and restore","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:kVxszfLPsBobDXaBZo4xqEZt8p5RjCaY9xZq5A7iq-A
1795385063289038	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3dh	{"description":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] Migrations are tested from every released schema version.\n- [ ] The oracle revocation path is exercised end to end: a revoked version makes its findings compute as `needs_review`, and old versions stay evaluable.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.md §6.7.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","surface"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"m","title":"M5.6 Test migrations and the oracle revocation path","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:pTCDsgoxI3ejW9qFdnbssk0wjdwuUfcw8Pc8UqeDNM0
1795385063313968	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-16w	{"description":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] The `scourge` and `scourge-verify` binaries are packaged.\n- [ ] Installation touches no global hooks or host trust settings (INV-43).\n- [ ] The display name, binary names and schema namespace live in one place, so a rename is routine.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.md (header, \"Codename\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","surface"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"s","title":"M5.7 Packaging","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:a57ygAQKnQHLg4eRyo7_el8uZusUGr0sVAgUA4a93po
1795385063338719	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ss	{"description":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every published claim has its numerator and denominator, per-family results, all attempts, invalid setups, timeouts and model refusals.\n- [ ] First-attempt success and the outcome of all attempts are reported separately.\n- [ ] An unrepresented workflow gets no cold-agent support claim.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §15.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m5","docs"],"parent":"bn-109","size":"s","title":"M5.8 Publish claims limited to what was measured","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:TjP_OH_Sbbk-6m-hTD76JnGkAwTux_1NYT54wVozVck
1795385063505418	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-38q	{"description":"# Context\nOn this machine `bn`, `edict`, `vessel`, `rite` and `ward` are installed. `teapot`, `hallpass`, `playpen`, `sippycup` and `investigation` are not on the PATH (plan §2.2). The inventory must keep installed identity apart from checkout identity.\n\n# Do\n- Build teapot, hallpass and playpen from their checkouts under ~/src and make them runnable for this project (pin with mise where it fits).\n- Write the inventory as a machine-readable file (for example docs/inventory.json): per tool the research pin from notes/plan.research.md §7, the checkout HEAD, the installed version string and the executable digest. An unknown commit is recorded as unknown.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The inventory file exists and a script regenerates it.\n- [ ] `teapot --version`, `hallpass --version` and `playpen --version` run, and their output is in the file.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2, §4.3 (instrument identity).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"s","title":"M0.1.1 Build the stack tools and record installed versus checkout identity"}	blake3:9bCTC_gEH8tgMySPrBW8jleG0-j9yBoAbh8Iswrxy4k
1795385063530834	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1iw	{"description":"# Context\nPlan §2.2 lists about 18 facts that shape the design, each with a consequence. Source was read, not run. Examples: Teapot draws Hallpass units in blocks and never refunds; Teapot can send a request and keep no flow; Playpen refuses loopback destinations; Teapot has no who-am-I; Teapot refuses to start enforced with a pass it cannot enforce; Teapot's own findings, confidence levels and `findings repro --verify`.\n\n# Do\nFor each row: run the tool and observe the behavior, or point at the exact source line if it cannot be run. Mark the row confirmed, changed or wrong, with the command and output or the file and line.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A table with one line per §2.2 row: verdict, evidence, and the Teapot/Hallpass/Playpen commit it was checked at.\n- [ ] Every \"changed\" or \"wrong\" row names the plan section it affects.\n\n# No claim\nA confirmed row holds for the pinned commit only. Teapot is mid-milestone and moving.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"m","title":"M0.1.2 Confirm every row of plan §2.2 against the running tools"}	blake3:FoJILtvJv_oiUouWd2miT_7zT2SWqi6dispvgfVYYUc
1795385063557341	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1b4	{"description":"# Context\nThe verifier needs nine operations from an HTTP instrument (plan §7.2): describe, check session, send one request under a named session (including a declared macro for a multi-step plant), read a flow by ID, dry run, units left, list jobs, cancel, and read the edit surface. No flag is guessed. M1.3 tests these against the real binary.\n\n# Do\n- Read `teapot schema` and record the exact argv and the JSON output shape for each operation.\n- For \"send\": find what reference Teapot offers by which a send can be looked up afterwards. If it offers none, note it for the upstream request in M0.7.4.\n- Save one real output per operation as a contract fixture under fixtures/contracts/.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A pinned argv table for all nine operations, each with a captured output from the pinned Teapot version.\n- [ ] Operations Teapot cannot do are listed as gaps, each with the feature it restricts.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"m","title":"M0.1.3 Pin exact argv for each instrument operation from `teapot schema`"}	blake3:l5qn3ypZAaztRf3Sfpy4vXYsZ5U1KVA2Z-I7eaiCrV0
1795385063586438	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dcn	{"description":"# Context\nTeapot now files findings of its own, with `tentative`, `firm` and `certain` confidence, `findings repro --verify`, a `fixed` state and an export. In a Scourge workspace all of that is a lead (plan §2.3, INV-48). `scourge lead add --from teapot:finding:N` needs to know Teapot's surface and which checks map to a Scourge class.\n\n# Do\n- List the `teapot leads` and `teapot findings` commands, the fields a finding carries, and what `teapot guide findings` teaches.\n- List Teapot's check IDs and mark the ones that map to `web-authz/private-object-read` or `web-authz/forbidden-state-change`. The mapping is data in the pack.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The surface listing and the mapping table, at the pinned Teapot commit.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2, §2.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"s","title":"M0.1.4 List Teapot's leads, findings and guide surface, with check IDs mapped to classes"}	blake3:CnpM46sJVOHW8nonFJi2ZBReXTQqbWp6Az8TXbrNdQY
1795385063613236	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3vk	{"description":"# Context\nThe verifier's channel to the target runs through an instrument the agent also uses. As read from source, Teapot's match-and-replace rules, intercept and Lua hooks run on its proxy listener and not on the replay client behind `requests send`, and a session adds its cookies, headers, CSRF tokens and, on a logged-out answer, one login and one retry of a GET or HEAD (plan §2.2, §3.4). Source was read and not run.\n\n# Do\nWrite tests against the real binary:\n- Add a match-and-replace rule, an intercept and a Lua request and response hook, then call `requests send`. Does any of them change the request sent or the response stored?\n- Send under a session whose login has expired. Record every extra request the session adds, and whether a write is ever retried.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A runnable test per case, with its observed result at the pinned commit.\n- [ ] If any rule or hook reaches a verifier send: a note for M0.7.4 to request a send mode that bypasses them and records that it did.\n\n# No claim\nA pass here does not remove the edit-surface check in the design. The verifier still reads the edit surface on every run (INV-49).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2, §3.4 (\"The verifier's channel\"), §7.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument","custody"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"m","title":"M0.1.5 Test whether a rule, intercept or hook can touch `requests send`, and what a session adds to a send"}	blake3:Y22EJ4yka3w26vjNdcu8an4K7qu3aN3dqOIVxB572ms
1795385063639554	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2cv	{"description":"# Context\nThe starting candidate for keeping verifier credentials away from the worker is that the verifier sends from a Teapot project of its own (plan §3.4). That only works if two Teapot projects can draw on one Hallpass pass.\n\n# Do\nCreate one pass and two Teapot projects. Send from both. Record what the ledger and each project's counters show.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A runnable test and its result: works, refused, or works with a caveat (for example double-drawn blocks).\n- [ ] The result is handed to the custody spike (M0.5.1).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §7.4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument","custody"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"s","title":"M0.1.6 Test whether two instrument projects can spend one pass"}	blake3:ugMVorBOLeIqSKX74u5Rwg9kSp2ronaKlWbmLJs--s4
1795385063665385	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-gox	{"description":"# Context\nThree copies of Hallpass are in use: HEAD 0.2.0, Playpen's pinned revision, and Teapot's vendored snapshot (plan §2.2). A pass that one copy cannot read breaks enforcement silently.\n\n# Do\nIssue one pass with Hallpass HEAD. Read it with Playpen and with Teapot. Record the digest each one computes.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A runnable check showing the same pass accepted by all three, with matching digests, or the exact incompatibility.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","instrument"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"s","title":"M0.1.7 Show one pass readable by all three Hallpass copies"}	blake3:LiF3Gib-LBr0WYVNb4zE0KQmn_3ryBwSQaVCV4P1EJg
1795385063692751	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1xb	{"description":"# Context\nPlan §11.4: use established crates for CLI parsing, serialization and schema generation, SQLite, hashing and process IO, and pin the versions in M0.1. Use Investigation's recording crate when it exists, otherwise its JSON protocol.\n\n# Do\nPick one crate per job, check each against the sibling tools' choices (Teapot, Hallpass and Investigation are Rust), and pin them in the workspace manifest created by M0.2.1.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The workspace `Cargo.toml` pins each dependency, with one line per choice in a short ADR or the manifest's comments.\n- [ ] `cargo deny` or an equivalent license and advisory check passes.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §11.4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","surface"],"parent":"bn-2ew","size":"s","title":"M0.1.8 Choose and pin the Rust dependencies"}	blake3:S4WMgQyfEcDH0NSiLnynq4lNtHuCobWLZDwakpgxmX4
1795385063720232	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3og	{"description":"# Context\nFirst code in the repository. The plan's layout (§11.1) is a Rust workspace with `crates/`, `packs/`, `fixtures/` and `evals/`. The fixture target is a small web application the oracles run against.\n\n# Do\n- Create the workspace and a `fixtures/target/` crate that builds one self-contained binary listening on a configurable address.\n- Accounts: alice and bob in different tenants, an admin, and a second account of each user role for the verifier (alice-verify, bob-verify). Separate verifier principals are how target isolation is achieved (plan §3.4), so the fixture needs four test accounts, not two.\n- Cookie-session login and logout, and an identity route `GET /app/api/me` whose JSON has a stable subject at `/id`.\n- `POST /app/_reset` to return to the seeded state, reachable only with an administration secret.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `cargo build` produces the fixture binary; `cargo test` covers login, logout and the identity route for every account.\n- [ ] Two different session labels for one account return the same subject (needed for fixture F-01).\n- [ ] Reset restores the seeded state.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6, §11.1.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.1 Cargo workspace and the fixture target skeleton: accounts, sessions, identity route"}	blake3:DTT1St5_s24A1S0QAA_kxi0OxVkyC28n82dVW9KPjbw
1795385063752942	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3n8	{"description":"# Context\nThe first oracle plants a 128-bit canary in a field the owner can write, then checks who can read it. The second oracle writes canaries into the same field. The fixture's resource must support exactly that, and the engagement example in the plan is written against it.\n\n# Do\nImplement `orders`, owned by the creating user:\n- `POST /app/api/orders` with a free-text `/note`, returning the new `/id`. Expose it as a Teapot macro named `create-order`.\n- `GET /app/api/orders/{id}` returning the note.\n- `PATCH /app/api/orders/{id}` setting `/note`.\n- `DELETE /app/api/orders/{id}`.\nCorrect behavior: only the owner may read, change or delete an order.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Tests show the owner can create, read, change and delete, and that with no flaw switched on another user and an anonymous caller cannot.\n- [ ] The create response establishes creation (it returns the ID of the object this request made), which cleanup depends on (INV-40).\n- [ ] The stanzas `[property.orders]` in plan §4.6 run unchanged against it.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6, §6.5, §6.6.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.2 Owner-private orders: create with a free-text note, read, write, delete"}	blake3:M4rflW36FwoTkCOV3rV87k0Owy-kLijLeGAJuNmECUc
1795385063778802	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24x	{"description":"# Context\nThe oracle measures who received the canary. Policy says whether they should have (D15). A public catalog and a private order with a missing check look the same on the wire. The fixture needs one correctly behaving resource for each audience so that the policy rules `owner`, `group`, `authenticated` and `public` can each be exercised (fixtures F-02, F-04, F-36).\n\n# Do\nAdd four resources with create, read and delete, each with a free-text field:\n- owner-only (for example `invoices`),\n- group-readable: readable by users in the owner's tenant,\n- authenticated-public: readable by any logged-in user,\n- public by design (`catalog`): readable with no login.\nAlso add a per-account setting that shares the owner's new objects with another user, which the worker can switch on as that owner (needed for fixture F-40).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Tests show each resource is readable by exactly its audience.\n- [ ] The sharing setting, once on, makes new objects readable by the named user.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §6.5, §14 (F-02, F-04, F-36, F-40).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.3 Policy controls: owner-only, group-readable, authenticated-public and public resources"}	blake3:otooTxm1tk5Hy-8Qe5iamXNmW6CYQNNyyfAS_H58pTU
1795385063800259	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1eh	{"description":"# Context\nThe fixture needs real violations for the oracles to find, each one a single missing check that can be switched off again (see M0.2.7).\n\n# Do\nPlant, each behind its own switch and on by default:\n- a cross-user read: another logged-in user can fetch an order by ID;\n- a cross-user write: another logged-in user can PATCH an order's note;\n- a missing authentication check on an owner-private route: an anonymous caller can read it (fixture F-36);\n- a shared cache that serves the owner's response to anonymous requests made after the owner's read (fixture F-37).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test per flaw shows the violation with the switch on and correct behavior with it off.\n- [ ] Each flaw is listed in the ground-truth file (M0.2.8) and nowhere the agent can read.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §6.6, §14 (F-36, F-37).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.4 Planted flaws: cross-user read, cross-user write, missing authentication"}	blake3:JEzm6EXY8e0I-6B6bNS4brYrzLAAsTHxT0CHYUkGWf0
1795385063830142	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ez	{"description":"# Context\nEach decoy is a tempting wrong conclusion. The fixture catalog pairs the right behavior with it (plan §14). A fixture with no decoys would only show that the oracle can say yes.\n\n# Do\nAdd routes or modes for:\n- a login or error page that returns 200 for a private object (F-03);\n- a cache that serves a stale victim response (F-06);\n- two session labels that resolve to one subject (F-01, uses M0.2.1);\n- a 403 response whose body contains the object (F-05: this one is a real exposure, and content decides, not status);\n- a route that echoes any recent canary on every response (F-08);\n- a session that expires or switches user mid-sequence (F-07).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test per decoy demonstrates the behavior with plain HTTP requests.\n- [ ] Each decoy is in the ground-truth file with the conclusion that would be wrong.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §14 (F-01, F-03, F-05 to F-08).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.5 Decoys: 200 login page, stale cache, same-user labels, 403 carrying data, canary echo"}	blake3:MIdNULfyz7murWcpW8vbo5x3WH-nVsP_TwsaF2Sg-3w
1795385063858867	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2oz	{"description":"# Context\nRecovery is tested on a state-changing request whose response is lost: the action commits and the connection drops (fixtures F-12, F-13; plan §7.5). `teapot-testbed` has such an endpoint; the Scourge fixture needs its own.\n\n# Do\nAdd a mode or route on the orders resource that performs the create or write and then closes the connection without a response. Make it switchable per request so a test can drop one chosen step.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test shows the object exists (or the write landed) after the dropped response.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5, §14 (F-12).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"s","title":"M0.2.6 An act-then-drop route"}	blake3:E-_DyW3jz4LX0NMPSGylNMcg4kpddbQI9pWBddiJ_Qg
1795385063886844	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-189	{"description":"# Context\nFix verification must tell a real fix from a \"fix\" that only broke the test (plan §6.8; fixtures F-15 to F-18, F-47). Teapot's testbed has `POST /app/_fix/{issue}`; the Scourge fixture follows that pattern.\n\n# Do\nFor each planted flaw (M0.2.4), an administration-only switch with these settings:\n- real fix: the missing check is restored;\n- partial fix: the reported route is fixed and a nearby variant still works (an alternate route to the same resource), and one variant that hides one canaried field and leaves another readable;\n- broken login: the \"fix\" breaks authentication for everyone;\n- removed route: the endpoint is gone.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test per setting shows the behavior with plain HTTP requests.\n- [ ] The switches need the administration secret and are not reachable with a user session.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.8, §14 (F-15 to F-18, F-47).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"m","title":"M0.2.7 Fix switches: real fix, partial fix, broken login, removed route"}	blake3:LzQgIdmChJu5GWdjb5TwccSVej1zWw3Z3-vyimHp5Vk
1795385063916195	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ag	{"description":"# Context\nThe fixture's planted flaws are documented in its own source, so an agent that can read the source has the answer key. The fixture runs as a prebuilt binary outside the agent's tree, with ground truth in a file the agent cannot read (plan row M0.2; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3).\n\n# Do\n- A ground-truth file listing every planted flaw, control and decoy with its expected verdict.\n- A build step that produces the binary and installs it, the ground truth and the administration secret somewhere outside the workspace an agent is given.\n- An example engagement file and Hallpass pass for the fixture. It must be the example printed in plan §4.6: declare a property for every resource class, the public ones included, and name no principal pair.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] From a fresh checkout, one command builds and starts the fixture from outside the tree.\n- [ ] The example engagement's requests all succeed against the running fixture.\n- [ ] The engagement declares every resource class, so it is not an answer key (notes/plan.evaluation.md §2, hint parity).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6; notes/plan.evaluation.md §2, §3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","fixture"],"parent":"bn-8be","size":"s","title":"M0.2.8 Ground truth, prebuilt packaging outside the agent's tree, and the matching engagement example"}	blake3:325ptIVbMFReF7yk7KCXXuQMsiyH5fs_ju00a6sriH8
1795385063953193	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-99s	{"description":"# Context\nEdict's `evals/` harness (in ~/src/edict) drives a real agent from a terse prompt that names no tool and carries no workflow, scores with scripted checks, self-tests with a scripted agent, and refuses to run if a live-environment safety check fails. Do not write a new runner (notes/plan.evaluation.md §3).\n\nThis machine has live `rite` hooks that spawn agents, so an unhermetic trial contaminates others.\n\n# Do\n- Bring the harness into `evals/` here (adapt, do not fork more than needed) with a Scourge task definition: start the fixture, prepare a workspace, run the agent, collect the transcript and final state.\n- Keep its safety check and extend it to refuse when `rite` hooks or other agent-spawning hooks are live in the trial environment.\n- Record for every run: model and harness version, the prompt, tool versions, what the agent was given, budgets, retries and human interventions.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] One end-to-end dry trial runs on the development fixture and writes a transcript and a run record.\n- [ ] The harness refuses to start with a live hook present.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §1, §3; notes/plan.md §2.2 (Edict row).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-284","size":"m","title":"M0.3.1 Adapt Edict's evals harness and make it hermetic"}	blake3:oqC4THmuP8O5T2duaQge15tR3QABtsYd52Rlz_kMTAU
1795385063992334	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3vt	{"description":"# Context\nGround truth, target reset, canary secrets and grading state must not be readable or writable by the assessed agent. This is the same boundary as the isolated custody tier (plan §3.4). It is built once here and qualified for custody in M0.5.\n\n# Do\n- Run the agent under a second Unix account or in a container whose only routes out are the fixture's port and, later, the broker socket and the instrument's proxy port.\n- Setup may need the operator, probably with root. Print the commands. Never run privileged setup automatically (INV-43).\n- Script three attempts from inside the boundary: read the ground-truth file, read the fixture's source, read the administration secret.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] All three scripted attempts fail, and the test output shows the refusals.\n- [ ] The agent inside the boundary can still reach the fixture and run its tools.\n- [ ] The setup commands are documented and reproducible.\n\n# No claim\nThis shows the three read attempts fail. The eight tamper attempts of the custody spike are M0.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3 (\"Referee boundary\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval","custody"],"parent":"bn-284","size":"m","title":"M0.3.2 Build the worker boundary: second account or container, with scripted read attempts that fail"}	blake3:tXiahjl0bRIKmkOcBXv8qgDbCrRCaJke4zG8dr5NCJo
1795385064028470	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2nt	{"description":"# Context\nNo model grades a trial. Scoring is scripted checks against real state and the ground-truth file. The harness has to prove its own scoring before any agent run is believed.\n\n# Do\n- Scoring for the baseline task: for each submitted finding, compare the claim with ground truth (true violation, decoy, or not in the key). A credible issue missing from the key goes to adjudication, not to \"false\" (fixture F-34).\n- A scripted correct agent that reports exactly the planted violations.\n- A scripted cheating agent that reports the decoys, or reads the answer where it can.\n- A no-instructions control run.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The scripted correct agent scores 100%.\n- [ ] The scripted cheating agent fails, for the stated reason.\n- [ ] The no-instructions control completes and is scored.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §10.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-284","size":"m","title":"M0.3.3 Scripted checks against real state, with correct-agent, cheating-agent and no-instructions self-tests"}	blake3:84M3HrOPlTxXjkQ3DKMTPls-QBrbkjQPsGE5VkrQitw
1795385064058907	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bu	{"description":"# Context\nCost per trial sets the spend cap and the corpus size for every later evaluation tier. The owner prefers a larger corpus wherever the budget allows, so this number decides how far past the floors the evaluation can go (notes/plan.evaluation.md §11).\n\n# Do\nRecord per run: tokens in and out, dollars, wall time, tool calls and target requests. Write them to the run record and a summary table.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A dry trial's run record carries all five numbers.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §11.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-284","size":"s","title":"M0.3.4 Record cost and duration per run"}	blake3:DsBfyOjjGc5gPKRBYSbCq6yyk8Yo_CXFF5ep1Jm_zOk
1795385064085982	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-30z	{"description":"# Context\nD13 says measure first, so the measurement needs a result that would change the plan. Before any baseline run, fix a number of unsupported authorization claims across arms A and B. If the baseline comes in at or under that number, and the taxonomy's main failures are elsewhere (missed discovery, setup friction, lost evidence), M0.7 writes an ADR that re-scopes M1 before the verifier is built. One candidate re-scope is to lead with supplied leads from Ward.\n\nTen runs cannot show that a rate is low: zero in ten still allows a rate near 26%. So the rule can move work. It cannot cancel the verifier alone.\n\n# Do\nCommit a short file with the number, what counts as an unsupported claim (a Teapot finding the agent relied on counts as its claim), and what happens on each side of the number. Use the premise rehearsal (M0.0) to choose a sensible number.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The rule is committed with a date earlier than the first baseline run.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1 (\"The M0 decision\"); notes/plan.evaluation.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-284","size":"xs","title":"M0.3.5 Write down the M0 decision rule before any baseline run"}	blake3:dUwNbrTlkNlPiLNsaWXep1_n-pQehH5AF9DuPv_2YJM
1795385064117659	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2wv	{"description":"# Context\nA and B must have the same base agent, instruments, model settings, target permissions, information, task and report expectations. \"Same information\" covers everything an arm can see. An input that points at the planted case is a supplied hint.\n\n# Do\n- The task text: assess cross-user access and report findings with evidence. It names no tool workflow.\n- Condition B's guidance: Scourge's methodology as plain prose (create your own object with a fresh random value, check identities are distinct, run the controls, separate measurement from policy).\n- The facts in the engagement (accounts, resource classes, their policy) in a form arms A and B can use.\n- Pin the Teapot version for the round and record it.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The materials are committed and frozen before the first run.\n- [ ] A written parity check: what each arm can see, and that nothing singles out the flawed resource.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-3ec","size":"s","title":"M0.4.1 Condition materials: task prompt, the condition B guidance, pinned Teapot, hint parity"}	blake3:ueZa8VKUFiAAQNtUb1qp3EDjZ_5HtIxDbQqpxjLtT4E
1795385064150221	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1or	{"description":"# Context\nThe scored baseline. Through the hermetic harness, inside the worker boundary, on the development fixture with its decoys.\n\n# Do\nRun conditions A and B, at least five runs each, more if the M0.3.4 cost numbers allow. Reset the fixture between runs and randomize the order.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every assigned run is in the ledger with its transcript, score and cost, including runs that failed or timed out.\n- [ ] The M0 decision rule (M0.3.5) was committed before the first run.\n\n# No claim\nThese numbers are small-sample. They choose what to build. They support no claim about how often agents are wrong.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-3ec","size":"m","title":"M0.4.2 Run the baseline on the development fixture: at least five runs per condition"}	blake3:WEzl9FBBQLq6_RHhBewPTXDHSxhiwkQRnloRn515di0
1795385064197084	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-mek	{"description":"# Context\nA fixture full of decoys measures how easily an agent is fooled by decoys. A run on a real application shows the rate without them. The M2 smoke runs reuse the same target (notes/plan.evaluation.md §5).\n\n# Do\n- Choose one open-source multi-user application with owner-private objects that carry a free-text field, and a flaw in a class one of the two oracles covers.\n- Stand it up locally with test accounts. Run one session per arm.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The target, its version and its setup are recorded so M2 can reuse it.\n- [ ] Two transcripts with the findings each arm submitted and what supported them.\n\n# No claim\nThe target is public and may be in a model's training data. Unscored, and no gate depends on it.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §4, §5 (\"Real-application smoke runs\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-3ec","size":"m","title":"M0.4.3 One unscored run per arm on a real open-source application with per-user objects"}	blake3:saJPTmKhftabxtElobVaIOVe1OFSdbfMpjRwg3Zv7IU
1795385064228683	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-93a	{"description":"# Context\nThe taxonomy is M0's main product: which false conclusions agents reached, at which step, how often. Every obligation and fixture in the plan is justified against it.\n\n# Do\nRead every transcript. For each unsupported or false claim, record the cause (same subject under two labels, public by design, login page, cache, status code read as proof, a Teapot finding taken as proof, and so on), the step, and the arm. Count missed discoveries, setup friction and lost evidence too. Add cost per trial, calls and tokens.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A committed taxonomy with counts per cause and per arm, each traceable to transcript lines.\n- [ ] A Teapot finding the agent relied on is counted as the agent's claim.\n- [ ] The count the decision rule needs is stated.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §4; notes/plan.md §12.1.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval"],"parent":"bn-3ec","size":"m","title":"M0.4.4 Write the failure taxonomy with counts and cost per trial"}	blake3:rymwIOkZ9Zu2WEbfinO9gupXOmbfV_A1T_ZMJsiirHw
1795385064260728	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3mx	{"description":"# Context\nThe plan's obligations (§5.4), invariants (§13) and fixtures (§14) were written before any measurement. An obligation that addresses no observed or well-documented failure needs a stated reason to stay.\n\n# Do\nGo through §5.4, §13 and §14 line by line against the taxonomy. Mark each keep, cut or add, with the observed failure or the documented reason. Apply the decision rule from M0.3.5 and write down the outcome: proceed with M1 as planned, or re-scope it.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] An ADR with a disposition for every obligation, invariant and fixture.\n- [ ] The M0 decision, with the count it rests on.\n- [ ] Cuts are applied to the bones: packages and tasks the decision removes are closed with a comment.\n\n# Why\nThis is where planning detail that nothing observed supports gets removed, instead of being built.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.4, §12.1, §13, §14; notes/plan.evaluation.md §4.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","eval","adr"],"parent":"bn-3ec","size":"m","title":"M0.4.5 ADR: keep, cut or add each obligation and fixture against the taxonomy, and record the M0 decision"}	blake3:Al77n74yu_XJ5BYVOCZ-jf3wZ3PCxSjdjXSjVPSVMBQ
1795385064295630	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-34o	{"description":"# Context\nThe starting candidate (plan §3.4): the worker explores through the instrument's proxy port, plus read-only queries over its own flows; the verifier sends from a Teapot project of its own, which the worker's proxy port and flow queries never touch. That keeps the pass ledger and the verifier's credentials outside the boundary.\n\n# Do\n- Inside the M0.3 boundary, give the worker the proxy port and nothing else of the instrument.\n- Outside it, create the verifier's Teapot project with the verifier accounts' sessions (alice-verify, bob-verify).\n- Use the M0.1.6 result on two projects spending one pass.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The worker can explore the fixture as alice and bob through the proxy.\n- [ ] A script outside the boundary can send as alice-verify and read the flow back.\n- [ ] What the worker can and cannot reach is written down.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4 (\"The verifier's channel\", \"The M0.5 spike\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","custody"],"parent":"bn-zja","size":"m","title":"M0.5.1 Stand up the candidate topology: confined worker, verifier in a second instrument project"}	blake3:7xxJEZLKKQe3Hc2G3eYNv1_PWU9rvNWLarwP1Hs4mSg
1795385064333911	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-rq8	{"description":"# Context\nA scripted adversary inside the worker boundary tries (plan §3.4):\n1. Write Scourge's database.\n2. Write the instrument's database.\n3. Read a fixture administration secret.\n4. Plant a result where the verifier will read it.\n5. Replace the `scourge-verify` binary.\n6. Use its exploration access before or during a run to change sharing on the owner's account or to race the verifier's object.\n7. Send under a verifier session, or replay credentials read from a verifier run's flows.\n8. Add an instrument rule, intercept or hook that edits a verifier send or its response.\n\n# Do\nWrite each attempt as a runnable script with a clear success condition. Stand-ins are fine where the real thing does not exist yet (a placeholder database file and binary), and say so.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Eight scripts, each reporting succeeded or refused with evidence.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §14 (F-32).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","custody"],"parent":"bn-zja","size":"m","title":"M0.5.2 Script the eight tamper attempts"}	blake3:S3IjvZD7sp-5SrtvwL6G77BuJ-cSZ5sYseamoLwYhjE
1795385064368607	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2cu	{"description":"# Context\nAll eight must fail in the isolated tier with separate principals. For shared custody and for shared principals, each is listed as possible or not, because reports must say plainly what those configurations do not prevent.\n\n# Do\n- Run all eight in: isolated custody with separate principals; isolated custody with shared principals; shared custody.\n- Run the sixth attempt twice, with and without the worker's instrument traffic held while a run is active. Keep the hold if the attempt succeeds without it.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A table: attempt by configuration, with the outcome and its evidence.\n- [ ] All eight fail in isolated custody with separate principals. If one does not, the topology changes and this is run again.\n\n# No claim\nThis qualifies the boundary against these eight scripted attempts on this host. It is not a proof against an adversary with other ideas.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §14 (F-32, F-40, F-49).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","custody"],"parent":"bn-zja","size":"m","title":"M0.5.3 Run the attempts in each configuration and record the outcomes"}	blake3:J8M2bwL7SafM3dhBa-ajb6fpSOwRTVl3xV-d2K6e6os
1795385064398788	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-qpi	{"description":"# Context\nThe development fixture listens on loopback. Playpen refuses loopback destinations, and Teapot refuses to start enforced with a pass it cannot enforce. So the fixture cannot run `egress_enforced` as the stack stands (plan §2.2, §7.3). `--no-enforce` is a choice the operator makes in the engagement file and is never suggested as a repair.\n\n# Do\nTry the candidates: bind the fixture to a non-loopback address listed in the pass; or run the fixture and the instrument together inside one Playpen offline sandbox.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] One topology runs with enforcement on and a request outside the pass is refused, or\n- [ ] an upstream issue is filed against Playpen or Teapot with a minimal reproduction, and the fixture stays labeled `unenforced`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.2, §3.4 (\"Enforcement is a separate label\"), §7.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","custody","instrument"],"parent":"bn-zja","size":"m","title":"M0.5.4 Find one enforced local topology, or file the blocker upstream with a reproduction"}	blake3:LayVGSJZwEEhKzzaoppJBgqYMtX7dDyzELQmWN64uRA
1795385064434590	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-12d	{"description":"# Context\nRequired before the relevant code ships (plan §12.3). Short: problem, choice, guarantees, limits, evidence, revisit trigger.\n\n# Do\nRecord: how a confined worker explores; where the verifier's sessions and flows live; which `target_isolation` values each configuration can record (`separate_principals`, `clean_instance`, `separate_principals_unconfined`, `shared_principals`); the enforced topology or its blocker; the outcome table from M0.5.3 as evidence.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The ADR is committed and cites the M0.5.3 table.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §12.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","custody","adr"],"parent":"bn-zja","size":"s","title":"M0.5.5 ADR: custody topology, target isolation, the verifier's channel and confined exploration"}	blake3:0aVHUP225c8fLG94LkahF9dgqic7OcPBP0VRdMReMGU
1795385064466537	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-cot	{"description":"# Context\nThe fixtures state what Scourge needs from an evidence store, independent of how Investigation implements it. They can be written before Investigation ships.\n\n# Do\nUnder `fixtures/contracts/`, write runnable cases for:\n- capture: open a run, attach artifacts with digests, finalize;\n- read by ID with digest and lineage, including a selected part of an artifact;\n- record: store an immutable derived artifact (manifest, experiment, verification record) with lineage;\n- idempotent retry: the same ID returns the same artifact;\n- crash recovery: a capture interrupted before finalize is recoverable with its partial artifacts;\n- immutability: an original cannot be overwritten, and a redaction creates a derivative with its own digest (INV-27).\nUse Investigation's canonical JSON rules, including duplicate-key rejection (INV-44).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The cases run against a small driver interface with one adapter slot for Investigation.\n- [ ] Each case states the plan section or invariant it enforces.\n\n# No claim\nPassing against an in-memory double proves nothing about Investigation. M0.6.2 is the conformance run.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §13 (INV-27, INV-44).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","evidence"],"parent":"bn-2lu","size":"m","title":"M0.6.1 Write the evidence contract fixtures"}	blake3:zcIAs_Pi5pDmBEYdcLnkDP6Oc9e6n1YnkTQkzlZ4Ilg
1795385064498538	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2og	{"description":"# Context\nThe conformance run itself. Waits for Investigation to ship its recording API (W07).\n\n# Do\nWire the adapter slot to Investigation's recording crate, or to its JSON protocol if the crate is not the public route. Run every fixture. Record the Investigation version.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every contract fixture passes against a named Investigation version through its public interface.\n- [ ] Each gap is filed as a named package in Investigation's tracker.\n- [ ] A documented blocker does not close this bone.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §12.1 (\"Prerequisite\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","evidence"],"parent":"bn-2lu","size":"m","title":"M0.6.2 Run the contract fixtures through Investigation's shipped public interface"}	blake3:a-7-gqNFKcXuwtZ6PS0Bc8urMzO3OKo0ltrKxtKYyNs
1795385064528319	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2yt	{"description":"# Context\nThe engagement file is the largest setup cost and the thing every lead resolves against. Its shape in plan §4.6 is a proposal until the baseline and the fixture have exercised it.\n\n# Do\nFreeze, as short ADRs with a machine-readable schema each:\n- the engagement file: `[engagement]`, `[principal.*]` with identity probe and optional verifier session, `[property.*]` with read, write, plant, remove, readers, writers and policy;\n- policy rules and their sources (`engagement`, `document`, `operator`, `inferred`);\n- the claim template: slots, recipe, controls with the alternative each rules out, oracle, cleanup, retest variants.\nApply the M0.4.5 dispositions.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Schemas committed, and the development fixture's example engagement validates against them.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §4.6, §6.2, §6.3, §12.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","adr"],"parent":"bn-1y7","size":"m","title":"M0.7.1 ADRs: engagement, property and claim-template schemas"}	blake3:te2NvhaYBtkFsG5dmi9fGzdKfmDOYn5TrOD41PlvnWE
1795385064556242	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-knf	{"description":"# Context\nThese are the contracts between lanes: the surface lane, the verifier lane and the evidence lane all read and write them.\n\n# Do\nFreeze, as short ADRs:\n- the JSON envelope (`scourge.response.v1`), the `next` argv rule and machine-mode behavior (plan §8.4);\n- error codes and the exit-code table, following Investigation's codes 2 to 7 and adding 8 for refusals (§8.5);\n- request-key semantics (§8.6);\n- the verification record's fields (§6.9), the per-engagement hash chain and the write-ahead publication intent (§9.3);\n- accounting evidence fields and the three `attribution` values (§7.4);\n- instrument argv pins from M0.1.3.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] ADRs committed, each with its evidence and a revisit trigger.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.9, §7.4, §8.4 to §8.6, §9.3, §12.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","adr"],"parent":"bn-1y7","size":"m","title":"M0.7.2 ADRs: response envelope, error and exit codes, request keys, record, chain and publication format"}	blake3:GS33ah_N0LuJWx6uAceh_YenLnGIQ58t5AEdJQlHym8
1795385064591426	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ggt	{"description":"# Context\n`web.private-object-read.v1` (plan §6.5) and `web.forbidden-state-change.v1` (§6.6) are specified in full so that nobody improvises their controls. Each lists its flows, what each rules out, the predicate, the invalid reason codes and the known misses.\n\n# Do\nCheck both specifications against the development fixture as built and the baseline taxonomy. Fix anything the fixture showed to be wrong. Freeze them, with the list of checks the mutation suite will remove one at a time.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Both specifications committed as frozen, each with its numbered list of checks.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5 to §6.7; notes/plan.evaluation.md §9.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","adr","verifier"],"parent":"bn-1y7","size":"s","title":"M0.7.3 ADR: freeze the two oracle specifications"}	blake3:pCP3Rz4rLHlQoZPLl7AkILCguPD8uRM2-JWF5vsfPZU
1795385064620097	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2r5	{"description":"# Context\nA blocker is work: if the instrument lacks an operation, add the smallest general capability upstream or restrict the feature (plan §12.4).\n\n# Do\nFile, each with a reproduction and the Scourge feature it gates:\n- Teapot: a per-send reference the verifier can look up after a lost response.\n- Teapot or Hallpass: run-level attribution of ledger units (a caller reference at spend time).\n- Playpen or Teapot: enforcement for a loopback target, if M0.5.4 found no topology.\n- Teapot: a send mode that bypasses rules, intercepts and hooks and records that it did, only if M0.1.5 found one that reaches a verifier send.\n- Investigation: schedule W07 first inside its milestone B, because M0.6 and M1.4 wait on it.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Each request is filed in the owning repository's tracker and linked here.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2, §7.4, §9.2, §12.2 (row M0.7).\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","upstream"],"parent":"bn-1y7","size":"s","title":"M0.7.4 File the upstream requests"}	blake3:s29Xc8I8DiYeoYvK8k2w17v9Cb7RAOprZn3VjUb9ycs
1795385064650124	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-73w	{"description":"# Context\nThe M1 bones were written from plan revision 5, before any measurement. The plan itself is frozen at revision 5 until M0 produces evidence.\n\n# Do\n- Update notes/plan.md once, as revision 6, from the M0.1 inventory, the M0.4.5 ADR and the frozen contracts. This is the only revision M0 produces.\n- Go through every M1 bone: close what the M0 decision cut, fix what the contracts changed, split anything that is now clearly too large.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every open M1 bone matches the frozen contracts.\n- [ ] Bones cut by the M0 decision are closed with a comment that names the ADR.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m0","plan"],"parent":"bn-1y7","size":"s","title":"M0.7.5 Re-cut the M1 bones against the frozen contracts and the M0 decision"}	blake3:G-k3TX6B4cuB_UUNiHD8zCpIgWiyG4TTMwFA1235ONk
1795385064682515	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-xwe	{"description":"# Context\nFive nouns for agents (engagement, lead, experiment, run, finding) and the internal objects of plan §4.2. IDs are opaque with a readable prefix (`L-`, `R-`, `V-`) and are never derived from titles, URLs or time.\n\n# Do\n- Types for lead and lead revision, lead event, run and step, experiment, verification record index, and the result dimensions of §5.2 as separate enums (run state, external effects, evidence completeness, oracle result, reproduction, cleanup, custody, target isolation, enforcement, policy source, applicability).\n- Canonical JSON with SHA-256, using Investigation's rules. Reject duplicate keys wherever a manifest, experiment or record is read (INV-44).\n- Secrets never appear in identifiers or summaries (INV-23): credentials are referenced by the instrument's session name.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Serialization round-trip tests, and a duplicate-key document is rejected.\n- [ ] Two executions always get two run IDs, even when every byte matches.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.1 to §4.3, §5.2; INV-23, INV-44.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-264","size":"m","title":"M1.1.1 Domain types, opaque IDs and canonical JSON"}	blake3:7iE-KTp5Zj_FkiHKqgoMSi66cMnouqyytmCouKv5PqU
1795385064717091	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1ca	{"description":"# Context\nSQLite in WAL mode with foreign keys and explicit migrations. Tables: `engagements`, `leads` (with revisions), `lead_events`, `runs` (with steps), `verifications` (index, one chain head per engagement, publication intents), `audit_events`. They hold operational metadata, references and receipts. No evidence blobs, no copy of Bones state, no Investigation lifecycle state (INV-29).\n\n# Do\n- Every connection sets `synchronous=FULL` and verifies the effective value (INV-26).\n- Every committing transaction advances one store-wide revision counter, so a reader can name the exact state it saw.\n- `audit_events` is written in the same transaction as the state change.\n- No `verified` column anywhere: status is computed on read (INV-02).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A schema test lists the allowed tables and columns and fails on any other.\n- [ ] A test shows the revision counter moves on every kind of write, including a lead event that moves no chain head.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.1; INV-02, INV-26, INV-29.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-264","size":"m","title":"M1.1.2 Coordination store: SQLite, migrations, one store-wide revision counter"}	blake3:8rWSonoHZ-dssV2EuHviXOlV5MkvOYISC3OQRysB1kg
1795385064747374	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ij	{"description":"# Context\nAgents never invent a request key. Each effectful command mints one, persists it with the run before any external effect, and prints it. This is what makes a lost response recoverable without repeating an action.\n\n# Do\n- Persist the run row and request key before any external call (INV-12).\n- Reusing a key with the same input returns the original run and never starts another. With different input it returns `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED` (INV-13).\n- An unknown key and another engagement's key get the same answer (INV-25).\n- Revision conflicts on lead edits return `REVISION_CONFLICT`.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Lost-response test: the command is killed after the run row is written, and the same key reopens the same run.\n- [ ] Key-reuse tests for same input and different input.\n- [ ] Revision-conflict test.\n- [ ] A crash test at the first boundary shows nothing external happened before the run row existed.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5, §8.6; INV-12, INV-13, INV-25.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-264","size":"m","title":"M1.1.3 Runs and request keys: persisted before any effect"}	blake3:q2Utio_GhcGU6CXeMFdAUXtnaY_xuMF43-Pd_oi9GGg
1795385064777502	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1wp	{"description":"# Context\nThe engagement says what is authorized: the pass, the principals, the declared properties with their policy rules, and the limits. It references the pass and does not restate what the pass binds.\n\n# Do\n- Parse and validate the engagement file against the schema frozen in M0.7.1.\n- Store each revision as an immutable manifest with its digest and the pinned pass digest. Amending the file creates a new revision.\n- Policy rules: audience (`owner`, `group`, `authenticated`, `public`) for reads and writes, with source. A rule counts only when the operator admitted it. An unset rule admits nothing.\n- A changed, expired or revoked pass stops new runs (INV-45).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The development fixture's example engagement loads; malformed files fail with a usage error that names the stanza.\n- [ ] `Policy.allows(rule, exposure, principals)` is a pure function with tests for all four audiences and both exposures.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §4.6, §11.3; INV-06, INV-45.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-264","size":"m","title":"M1.1.4 Engagement manifest: parse, validate, revise, pin the pass"}	blake3:rDcAksZgfamIPMbwAjy_ohxSDYAD31wcvPbVH08n_2U
1795385064805957	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-dip	{"description":"# Context\nA reviewer routinely runs the CLI inside someone else's source tree. A `scourge.toml` found by walking up from the current directory selects a workspace and grants nothing. Executable paths, the evidence root, credential sources and sockets come only from an operator binding written by `scourge init` outside any inspected tree (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/scourge/`), keyed by workspace identity, root path and configuration digest.\n\n# Do\n- `scourge init PATH --engagement FILE` creates the workspace and the binding. The binding file is written atomically (temporary file, flush, rename, directory flush).\n- Without a valid binding the CLI shows its embedded orientation and the binding status, and invokes nothing.\n- `--workspace` or the nearest `scourge.toml` locates a workspace. With more than one engagement, case-specific commands need `--engagement`. No implicit \"last\" object for mutations (INV-46).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-28: a nested `scourge.toml` naming a sentinel adapter and a symlinked evidence root causes no adapter invocation, host probe, secret resolution or foreign read.\n- [ ] Selection tests with two engagements.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §8.7, §9.1; INV-21, INV-46; F-28.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2c6","size":"m","title":"M1.2.1 Workspace selection, the operator binding and `init`"}	blake3:CC_bOD6eRKxM14kArCgWzVIjKTsvN3larMHP-BPh_Sg
1795385064835104	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1aw	{"description":"# Context\nFollow the stack's conventions: `--format text|pretty|json`, a hidden `--json` alias, the `FORMAT` variable, and a JSON object envelope with an `advice` array. Agents branch on `result` and `error.code`, not on the exit code.\n\n# Do\n- The `scourge.response.v1` envelope as frozen in M0.7.2.\n- In machine mode every outcome is one JSON object on stdout, including usage and parse errors raised before a workspace is known. Unavailable identifiers are `null`. No prompts, pager, browser or logs on stdout (INV-32).\n- An error says what failed, whether anything was committed, whether an external effect may have occurred, and the next valid step as an argv with its effects.\n- One enumeration of error codes generates the exit-code table (exit 0, 2 to 8). A read that reports a blocker exits 0 with the code in its result.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Machine-mode tests for a parse error, an unknown workspace and a refusal: each is one JSON object.\n- [ ] A test asserts every error code has a row in the generated table, and `--json` equals `--format json`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.4, §8.5; INV-32.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2c6","size":"m","title":"M1.2.2 Response envelope, machine mode, and the generated error and exit-code table"}	blake3:kPxG-vmWC1GTKCXAIJhYQFe-X-yy5k9b8KZxS2kdCLU
1795385064862702	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-24v	{"description":"# Context\nBare `scourge` is read-only, works anywhere and fits in 2 KiB. Inside a bound workspace it shows state: the engagement, counts, the Teapot findings and leads not yet recorded here, the principals, properties and classes a lead needs, and one next action. `tldr`, `status`, `brief` and `onboard` are aliases.\n\n# Do\n- The entry screen of plan §8.1, with omission counts and drill-down routes when something does not fit.\n- A mandatory caveat is never dropped to fit the budget: shorten or withhold the claim (INV-35).\n- A run with unknown effects, pending cleanup or a broken chain takes the `Next` line ahead of everything else.\n- A lint over catalog strings: Scourge's own objects are named only with the five nouns, never with a synonym from the fixed list (candidate, hypothesis, issue, attempt, case, verdict, conclusion), and an instrument's object is written \"Teapot finding\" or \"Teapot lead\" (INV-33).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Size tests: bare `scourge` and default `status` each fit in 2 KiB at 0, 10 and 10,000 leads (INV-30).\n- [ ] Truncation tests keep the caveat.\n- [ ] The lint runs in CI and fails on a synonym.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.4, §8.1; INV-30, INV-33, INV-35.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2c6","size":"m","title":"M1.2.3 Entry screen and `status` within 2 KiB, with the five-noun lint"}	blake3:ILpFlouNxIZb0duu9Tp1xoueWkx8zIzZYqP3jIRQybg
1795385064898357	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-r3b	{"description":"# Context\n`show ID [--depth title|summary|full]` works for leads, findings, runs, records and instrument flow IDs. `list [leads|findings|runs]` is the bounded listing the entry screen's omission counts point to.\n\n# Do\n- Implement both as short read transactions.\n- Target content in any output is delimited as untrusted and size-bounded. This is hygiene, not a boundary. Raw evidence is opt-in.\n- A network-sink test over the whole read-only surface: status, help, `show`, `list`, `next`, `--plan`, `doctor` (INV-15).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `show` and `list` tests on a seeded store.\n- [ ] The network-sink test fails if any read-only command opens a connection to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.3, §8.4; INV-15, INV-22.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2c6","size":"m","title":"M1.2.4 `show` and `list`, and the proof that reads send no target traffic"}	blake3:BDBq6ekegXZw-ywlG_kGS1sl1Aeq0CwvpizCb9-Xxdo
1795385064928426	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-23h	{"description":"# Context\nEvery `next` entry is a complete argv that runs from the caller's directory. It carries the workspace and engagement whenever resolution depends on them, and in machine mode it names the format, because a new process with no `FORMAT` set would answer in text (INV-31).\n\n# Do\nA test harness that collects every suggested argv the CLI prints, then launches each exactly as printed: in a new process, with `FORMAT` unset, from an unrelated directory whose path contains spaces, in a workspace with two engagements.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every reply parses as one JSON object.\n- [ ] The harness fails if a command prints a suggestion it did not exercise.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.4; INV-31.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-2c6","size":"s","title":"M1.2.5 Launch every suggested argv in a new process"}	blake3:GR9MeM7alvBTxUfPP2LJNy-6-c90uMn6NbWUrytnFSs
1795385064958306	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20h	{"description":"# Context\nThe read-only half of the instrument contract.\n\n# Do\n- A subprocess layer: resolved executable from the operator binding, argv array, cleaned environment, JSON parsed strictly. Native exit codes are retained separately.\n- Describe: version, engine identity, enforcement state, protocol limits. The engine that answers must be the one recorded.\n- Check session: logged in, logged out or unknown, per principal.\n- Dry run: request count and whether it is within the ceiling.\n- Units left, and list jobs for a window.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real pinned Teapot for each operation.\n- [ ] An engine-mismatch test: the adapter refuses when the answering engine is not the recorded one.\n- [ ] A missing binary is `DEPENDENCY_UNAVAILABLE`, never a pass.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","instrument"],"parent":"bn-1st","size":"m","title":"M1.3.1 Structured invocation layer, with describe, check session, dry run, units left and list jobs"}	blake3:gwhdiggBC8j26QWO_6vF02GhQ1sjMVJRdK-wJtT6w4Q
1795385064986706	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3s5	{"description":"# Context\nThe effectful half. Every recipe flow is one request sent under a named session, then read back by flow ID as original bytes.\n\n# Do\n- Send one request under a named session, with the per-send reference M0.1.3 found (or the gap recorded).\n- Run a declared macro for a multi-step plant.\n- Read a flow by ID: original request and response bytes, timing, destination.\n- A login or retry the instrument adds to a step is recorded as a flow of that step.\n- Cancel means request, then observe.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real binary on the development fixture for each operation.\n- [ ] A send with capture off or lost returns \"no flow\", and the adapter does not report that as \"not sent\" (INV-41).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2, §7.5; INV-41.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","instrument"],"parent":"bn-1st","size":"m","title":"M1.3.2 Send under a named session, read a flow by ID, run a plant macro, cancel"}	blake3:PgIKOQWkpfCtVb8KyEMeUd63Q3r6pH4vSUa5T5IeWKk
1795385065013814	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-g2i	{"description":"# Context\nRules, intercepts, script hooks and session behaviors in the instrument could change a verifier send or the response stored for it. The verifier reads that edit surface at run start and run end and keeps it in the record. Enforcement is a separate label: each run records `egress_enforced` or `unenforced` as the instrument reports it.\n\n# Do\n- Read the edit surface. If anything could change a verifier send or its stored response, refuse with `INSTRUMENT_EDITS_ACTIVE`, unless the instrument offers a send mode that bypasses them and records that it did.\n- When the engagement requires enforcement and the instrument cannot enforce the pass, refuse with `ENFORCEMENT_UNAVAILABLE`. No flag or retry downgrades it (INV-17).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] With an active match-and-replace rule on the verifier's path, the run is refused before any send.\n- [ ] F-26: enforcement required and unavailable is refused, with no downgrade offered.\n- [ ] A test for Teapot's `pass-not-enforceable` refusal.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §7.2, §7.3; INV-17, INV-49; F-26, F-49.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","instrument","custody"],"parent":"bn-1st","size":"m","title":"M1.3.3 Edit surface and enforcement: refuse a run that could be altered or cannot be enforced"}	blake3:5l_Qb63A_k20j1P4GMKS_j4EXRTp84vQc-AwjMQ_QFI
1795385065044623	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-14o	{"description":"# Context\nThe instruments own accounting (D9). Scourge never debits a budget. Teapot draws units in blocks and never refunds, its counter includes spending from outside Teapot, and a block drawn in one run can pay for a later one. So a change in the counter is not one run's charge.\n\n# Do\nPer run, capture: pass ID and digest, instrument job IDs, the dry-run ceiling, the counters before and after (each may be absent), where each came from, its granularity and the window. `attribution` is `exclusive` only with positive evidence covering every spender, `shared` when another spender was seen, and `unestablished` otherwise, which is the usual value.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-44: two saved counter observations with the same job list and the same change, one including an outside spender; an absent counter; a block that pays for two jobs. All give `unestablished` and no per-run charge.\n- [ ] An absent counter stays unknown and is never reported as zero.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.4; INV-16; F-13, F-44.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","instrument"],"parent":"bn-1st","size":"s","title":"M1.3.4 Accounting evidence: record what the instrument reports and never debit"}	blake3:ahavbATvdrl6TuOGQdVhi6LPgKWQ2DfAFrTpPzKe-1M
1795385065074701	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-tnk	{"description":"# Context\nA claim template belongs to a pack and is compiled into the verifier. Agents do not author controls. Slot values from a lead are untrusted. The slots are `owner`, `other`, `read`, `plant` and `remove`. A lead that names a declared property takes `read`, `plant` and `remove` from it and supplies the principal pair.\n\n`plant` and `remove` run with the owner's credentials. A plant request written by the agent can create the object already shared with `other`, and then every control passes on a false claim.\n\n# Do\n- `Templates.resolve(lead, engagement)` returns an experiment or the missing slots. The record says who supplied each slot.\n- `--slot` cannot override a slot the property fixes (`SLOT_CONFLICT`).\n- A `read` request supplied by a lead must use GET or HEAD, or the run is refused with `METHOD_REFUSED` and nothing is sent (INV-37).\n- An experiment whose owner-side requests came from a lead is marked so status can cap it at `conditional` (INV-36).\n- Freeze the experiment with a digest that binds template version, resolved slots, principal bindings, oracle version and limits, and not the canary.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-38: a lead-supplied plant gives at most `conditional`, announced by `--plan`; a slot override is `SLOT_CONFLICT`.\n- [ ] F-39: a DELETE or POST bound to the read slot is `METHOD_REFUSED`, and a network sink sees nothing.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.2; INV-36, INV-37; F-38, F-39.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2w2","size":"m","title":"M1.5.1 Claim template and slot resolution for `web-authz/private-object-read`"}	blake3:fRStYay6LXaaCpKvb1cxJ4ZW5vTLcYxK_dH4rxSvGkw
1795385065101374	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-10s	{"description":"# Context\nA verify run follows ten steps (plan §6.1): persist the run row; take the run lock for the pass and the conflict groups for the principals' sessions; check engagement revision, pass and instrument; freeze and store the experiment; open an evidence run and record the accounting counters and the highest flow ID; send each round's flows; evaluate the oracle over those flows only; clean up; publish the record; finalize.\n\n# Do\n- A separate binary. No model call. It takes no observation or result from a caller (INV-01, INV-03).\n- Canaries: 128 random bits as 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, one per declared field per object per round, never reused (INV-20).\n- Persist each step's intent (step number, request digest, flow-ID floor, send reference) before sending, and its outcome after.\n- Read each flow back by ID and store the original bytes through the evidence bridge.\n- Identity probes at the start and end of every round.\n- Cleanup removes only objects whose creation by this run the plant's own response established. When creation is uncertain nothing is deleted and cleanup stays owed (INV-40).\n- Runs against one pass are serialized (INV-18).\n- The oracle registry is compiled in. A crash in the instrument or the oracle is `error`, never `false` and never `true` (F-14).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A complete round on the development fixture with the cross-user read switched on, with every flow stored and read back.\n- [ ] Two concurrent runs on one pass do not overlap.\n- [ ] F-27 cleanup cases: failed cleanup; plant response lost; an object with the same name that existed before the run survives.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.1, §7.5 to §7.7; INV-01, INV-03, INV-18, INV-20, INV-40; F-14, F-27.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2w2","size":"l","title":"M1.5.2 The `scourge-verify` run loop: lock, checks, canaries, step intents, flow read-back, cleanup"}	blake3:MaMs6Q8eJu2opVgu-fJcFoO1sWcL9KrEy5r65D3d2BI
1795385065129486	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-20u	{"description":"# Context\nThe oracle consumes the verifier's own typed observations for one round and returns `true`, `false`, `invalid` with reason codes, or `error`. Flows: identity probes I1, I2; plants P, P2 (two new objects, each with its own canaries); F0 anonymous read before the owner's; F1 owner read; F2 other-user read; F3 anonymous read; F4 other user reads the second object; identity probes I3, I4; cleanup C.\n\n# Do\n`Oracle.evaluate(observations) -> result`, pure:\n- `true`: the first object's canary is in F1 and absent from F4, the subjects are distinct and unchanged, and the canary is in F0, F2 or F3. Exposure is `unauthenticated` when it is in F0 or F3, otherwise `cross_principal`. Note `after_owner_read` when F0 lacks it and F3 has it.\n- `false`: setup valid and the canary absent from F0, F2 and F3.\n- `invalid`, never `false`: `SAME_SUBJECT`, `OBSERVER_INVALID`, `CANARY_NOT_SPECIFIC`, `SESSION_DRIFT`, `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- Matching runs over the decoded body. Transfer and content codings are undone under a size and ratio limit and the decoder is named. A body over the limit is `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- HTTP status is not an input (INV-08). The oracle never reads a policy rule.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Unit tests for each outcome and each reason code on saved observations.\n- [ ] The function's signature has no status-code field and no policy input.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.4, §6.5; INV-07, INV-08; F-42.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2w2","size":"m","title":"M1.5.3 Oracle `web.private-object-read.v1` as a pure function"}	blake3:5K5m3SRDIIfqRYvD_SLqsv78VuHc4Ot7JDuf_3q9rSk
1795385065162640	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-36n	{"description":"# Context\nEach fixture pairs the right behavior with a tempting wrong conclusion. They are runnable cases under `fixtures/oracle/` and regression tests from here on.\n\n# Do\nImplement as end-to-end cases against the development fixture, through the real verifier and adapter:\nF-01 same subject under two labels; F-02 public object, with and without a rule; F-03 200 login page; F-05 403 carrying the canary; F-06 stale cache; F-07 session drift; F-08 canary echo; F-09 a canary or flow from an earlier run; F-10 agent-supplied flow IDs are ignored; F-11 missing or truncated flow; F-14 instrument or oracle crash; F-36 owner-only property with no authentication check; F-37 shared cache after the owner's read; F-38, F-39 (from M1.5.1); F-42 compressed and escaped canary, and an oversized body.\nF-04 needs the status function and is finished in M1.6.3.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every listed case runs in CI against the real fixture and passes with the result plan §14 requires.\n- [ ] No case passes by being skipped: a missing dependency fails the suite.\n\n# No claim\nPassing fixtures shows the oracle handles these cases. Whether each check is doing work is the mutation suite's job (M1.5.5).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §14.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier","fixture"],"parent":"bn-2w2","size":"m","title":"M1.5.4 Runnable fixture cases for the first oracle"}	blake3:sZSkb_vvRbOdR2qKeHnhQZGIgq7u2MwFdsJ-qC06Cr8
1795385065191020	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-26a	{"description":"# Context\nThe oracle specification lists its checks: each identity comparison, each control, each canary correlation, each completeness test. The mutation suite removes one at a time and runs the fixture catalog. This is the strongest soundness evidence the project can produce cheaply, because it is mechanical and repeatable. An oracle version is registered only when it passes.\n\n# Do\nBuild the mutation mechanism (a switch per listed check that disables it) and a runner that reports, per mutant, which fixtures failed.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every listed check of `web.private-object-read.v1` is killed by at least one fixture: 100%.\n- [ ] A surviving mutant is resolved either way: a fixture is added, or the check is removed as doing nothing.\n- [ ] A mutant that removes the status-code independence (makes status an input) is killed by F-03 or F-05.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.7; notes/plan.evaluation.md §9; INV-08, INV-09.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-2w2","size":"m","title":"M1.5.5 Mutation suite: removing any one listed check fails a fixture"}	blake3:ofhGZZrP3RzCgy6juqWIrtxKzSwALbqOPQGojgVsGLY
1795385065221802	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3gx	{"description":"# Context\nA lead is anything unverified and costs one call. It needs only text, or only `--from`. Flow and Teapot references are hints, never evidence.\n\n# Do\n- `lead add TEXT [--flow ID] [--from teapot:finding:N|teapot:lead:N] [--class C] [--property P] [--slot k=v] [--policy TEXT]`.\n- Given `--flow` and no class: read that flow's session name and route from the instrument, match them against the declared properties, and put a filled-in `verify --plan` in `next`.\n- Given `--from`: take the title, endpoint and evidence flow IDs from Teapot and map the check to a class where the pack declares one. Copy nothing from a response body. Send no traffic.\n- `lead set`, `lead withdraw`, `lead defer`, `lead duplicate` as revisions and attributed, append-only events. Material fields are class and slots.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A text-only lead is created in one call.\n- [ ] An imported Teapot finding becomes a lead with origin `teapot`, and its confidence is shown as Teapot's claim.\n- [ ] A network sink shows `lead add` sends nothing to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §4.1, §8.3.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"m","title":"M1.6.1 `lead add` and the lead dispositions, including import from Teapot"}	blake3:s-aafslMDCayzCI93r2uk96SfZfm3_Q0ohkZlXSLDmE
1795385065255388	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2f7	{"description":"# Context\nOne immutable record per run, with the fields of plan §6.9. Each engagement has its own chain: a record contains the digest of the one before it. Publication is write-ahead so that recovery never puts a record on the chain that the verifier did not write.\n\n# Do\n- Before the verifier stores a record it persists a publication intent: engagement, run, record digest and the head it expects.\n- Then, in one transaction: link the record once for that run, check the head is still the expected one, advance it, mark the intent complete.\n- If the head has moved, nothing is linked, and the verifier rebuilds the record on the new head under a new intent.\n- `scourge run reconcile` finishes only a publication with a matching intent. An import cannot create an intent.\n- A record-shaped artifact with no intent is not on the chain and is ignored by status.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-20: an imported record-shaped artifact and a lookalike offered to `run reconcile` are never linked; one genuine pending publication reconciled twice at once yields one record and one head.\n- [ ] A broken chain is `INTEGRITY_FAILURE`.\n\n# No claim\nIn shared custody the chain detects accidents and casual edits. It does not stop a determined same-account forger, and reports say that.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.9, §9.3; INV-01, INV-28; F-20.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"m","title":"M1.6.2 Verification record, per-engagement hash chain and write-ahead publication"}	blake3:RDAKF1BUjlSvrWFAqqJaCe3aR37FgpaoLHZr3ZuSMx0
1795385065284629	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2el	{"description":"# Context\nEverything a read says about a lead comes from one pure function over the on-chain records that name it. There is no stored `verified` flag and no promotion transaction. It works in three steps: judge each record on its own (`broken`, `stale`, `stands`), pick the status record S (the latest true `verify` record) and the deciding record D, and read the answer off them. Records are ordered by chain sequence, never by a timestamp.\n\n# Do\n`Status.compute(lead, snapshot, audience) -> status, applicability, S, D`, exactly as plan §5.1:\n- status: `integrity_failure`, `lead` (with sub-state and last outcome), `refuted`, `needs_review`, `disputed`, `conditional`, `verified`. Integrity checks come first.\n- the rule that applies to S is the admitted rule in force at its run, or the first rule admitted for that resource afterwards. So a `conditional` finding becomes `verified` with no new run.\n- applicability from D: `as_observed`, `still_violated`, `fixed_within_tested_scope`, `unestablished`.\n- re-evaluate the oracle over retained originals; a mismatch or a missing original is `broken`.\n- the audience rule: for `third_party`, only records acquired in isolated custody qualify. M1 needs the function to take the parameter; the third-party cases are exercised in M2.5.\n- the retraction branch exists only where the shipped Investigation offers the query.\nA memo by record, oracle and rule digests is a cache and never an input.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Property tests over generated record histories.\n- [ ] F-04: one saved true observation gives `verified`, `conditional`, `conditional`, `refuted` under the four policy situations.\n- [ ] F-21 revoked oracle version is `needs_review`. F-22: class or slot change is `needs_review`; text change changes nothing; rule admission gives `verified` with no new run.\n- [ ] F-23 an edited stored verdict or memo is `INTEGRITY_FAILURE`. F-24 a missing original is `INTEGRITY_FAILURE`.\n- [ ] No table or file stores status or applicability.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.6, §11.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-50; F-04, F-21 to F-24.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","verifier"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"l","title":"M1.6.3 `Status.compute`: the pure status and applicability function"}	blake3:sLj882i3fF_qrIxVj20KKjiIgQA_ZqJjR3LaFEljubs
1795385065316467	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-23g	{"description":"# Context\n`verify` and `retest` are the only porcelain commands that send target traffic. Each says what it will do, and its `--plan` form shows it first and sends nothing.\n\n# Do\n- `verify LEAD --plan`: the matched template, the resolved slots and who supplied each, any unfilled slot as a form, the flows and their count, the effects, the request ceiling from the dry run, the custody, target isolation and enforcement the run would have, and the highest status the result can reach with the policy rule as it stands. For a class with no template: `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE`, the supported classes, and that the lead stays a lead.\n- `verify LEAD [--rounds N]`: the ten-step run. Two rounds by default; two true rounds is `reproduced`.\n- No command, file, import or API accepts a verdict, an oracle result or a record from a caller (INV-01).\n- An instrument's own confidence, verification state, fixed state or export is never an input (INV-48).\n- Only the operator admits a policy rule; a lead can propose one (INV-39).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] End to end on the fixture: lead, `--plan`, `verify`, and `show` reads `verified` with the full badge (INV-05).\n- [ ] F-48: promoting a Teapot lead, running `teapot findings repro --verify` until it says `fixed`, and exporting Teapot's report change no Scourge status and no applicability.\n- [ ] A catalog schema test shows no command has an input for a verdict.\n- [ ] `--plan` sends nothing (network sink).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §6.1, §8.2, §8.3; INV-01, INV-05, INV-15, INV-39, INV-48; F-48.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface","verifier"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"m","title":"M1.6.4 `verify` and `verify --plan`, with instrument conclusions kept inert"}	blake3:bwgw_7EBDdk1PqzP0b8LR_2a3nSbiHPEyzmzYBQMA9I
1795385065351696	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-25j	{"description":"# Context\nA read copies the rows it needs and the store revision inside one short read transaction, releases it, and only then reads evidence and re-evaluates oracles. A reader held open across a slow export would stop the write-ahead log from being checkpointed.\n\n# Do\n- Every read names its snapshot: store revision, chain checkpoint, oracle registry version.\n- A `current` label requires that the stores and registry still match after collection, checked with fresh reads, with a bounded number of retries. Otherwise the read presents the last complete named snapshot as history, or says no coherent read could be assembled.\n- The workspace read lists every standing true record with its tier, so a later, weaker run does not hide an earlier, stronger one (INV-50).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-43 (second half): an objection committed while a slow read is collecting evidence either appears, or the read names the older snapshot. It is never presented as the newer state without the objection.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.1, §10.3; INV-02, INV-42, INV-50; F-43.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","surface"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"s","title":"M1.6.5 Coherent snapshot reads: an objection committed during a slow read cannot vanish"}	blake3:k5fQDYEDoF9I3EuRXuoC2Ymkdz1H2w5B8-RJ8mUePu4
1795385065382574	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2dq	{"description":"# Context\nEach family ships with its vulnerable build, at least one fix and one partial fix, functional checks and a ground-truth file. Variation axes: authentication mechanism (cookie session, bearer token, API key), tenancy model (per-user, per-tenant, hierarchical), flaw placement (object read, object write, list endpoint, nested resource, alternate route), decoy set, and state shape (plain CRUD, multi-step creation). Renaming IDs in one solution does not make a second family.\n\n# Do\nDefine the package layout (how to start the target, its accounts, the engagement facts, ground truth, the fix switches, functional checks) and a checklist for packaging a candidate. Use the development fixture as the first package.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The development fixture is packaged in the format and runs through the harness from it.\n- [ ] The format keeps ground truth and administration secrets outside what the agent can read.\n\nOwner: not the implementing agent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §5 (\"Scenario factory\").\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","eval","fixture","separate-owner"],"parent":"bn-14c","size":"m","title":"M1.10.1 Fixture packaging format and the factory method"}	blake3:F9VD8x_yriLVeluZBrWqB8FmHz5G_XA_20iw7Tnyh_E
1795385065410967	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-f1x	{"description":"# Context\nThree sources (notes/plan.evaluation.md §5):\n- Historical authorization CVEs in open-source applications, with the vulnerable and the fixed release. Good for paired retests and supplied leads. Public knowledge, so never a discovery case. BOLABuster's results are a starting list, and not every one fits (the Grafana flaw is a delete).\n- One-check mutations of open-source multi-user applications: remove or weaken one ownership check. Restoring it is the fix; restoring it on one route only is the partial fix. The cheapest way to grow the corpus.\n- RealWorld backends: one API specification with many implementations. Reads are public by design, so they give write-side families and policy decoys.\n\n# Do\nBuild a candidate list of at least 20 with, per candidate: source type, the resource with a free-text field, the flaw, which oracle could verify it, and the tracks it suits (discovery, supplied lead, negative, fix verification, recovery).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A candidate list that covers every decoy class in the catalog plus real read and write violations and fix cases.\n- [ ] Each candidate is marked with whether one of the two oracles can state its violation.\n\nOwner: not the implementing agent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.evaluation.md §5.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","eval","fixture","separate-owner"],"parent":"bn-14c","size":"m","title":"M1.10.2 Source and screen candidate targets"}	blake3:qvqZ9ehgtatMFruPBMjwRxijSJxaHRA5vAlHE1TvfRI
1795385065438783	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1y5	{"description":"# Context\nThe packaged set the Tier 1 evaluation runs on.\n\n# Do\nPackage the chosen candidates in the M1.10.1 format. Check each against a shipped oracle before it is packaged: the vulnerable build must give `true`, the fixed build must not, and the functional checks must pass on both. Record the hours each family took.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] 10 to 12 packaged fixtures, covering every catalog decoy class plus real read and write violations and fix cases.\n- [ ] Each has accounts, ground truth, fix and partial-fix variants and functional checks.\n- [ ] Authoring cost per family is in the ledger.\n- [ ] The implementing agent has had no access to them.\n\nNeeds the first oracle (M1.5) for the screening step. More than 12 if the cost allows.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.2 (row M1.10); notes/plan.evaluation.md §5, §11.\n","kind":"task","labels":["m1","eval","fixture","separate-owner"],"parent":"bn-14c","size":"l","title":"M1.10.3 Package 10 to 12 Tier 1 fixtures and record the authoring cost"}	blake3:dxdoEjsmP38wRwnIxjwHaeYAbBm-Kz8gMGVUdhfdB8M
